// Generated by tools/build-why.js. Ready-made explanations for "Why, exactly?", keyed by place|what you said.
window.WHY = {"noodles|我要一个牛肉面。":{"why":"Chinese nouns need a measure word between the number and the thing, and the measure word usually describes the container or shape. Noodle soup is served in a bowl, so 碗 wǎn is the natural one: 一碗牛肉面 yì wǎn niúròu miàn. Plenty of locals do say 一个 yí ge when ordering in a hurry, so you weren't wrong, but 一碗 sounds like someone who eats here. Notice the tone change too: 一 is said yì before the third-tone 碗 wǎn, but yí before 个 gè.","tip":"Picture the steaming bowl in front of you: if it comes in a bowl, it's 碗 wǎn."},
"noodles|我是牛肉面。":{"why":"是 shì works like an equals sign: A 是 B means A and B are the same thing, so 我是牛肉面 wǒ shì niúròu miàn really does claim you are a bowl of noodles. English lets 'I'm the beef noodles' mean 'my order is the beef noodles', but Chinese doesn't stretch 是 that way when ordering. Use an action verb instead: 我要 wǒ yào, I want, or the slightly softer 来一碗 lái yì wǎn, bring a bowl.","tip":"是 is an equals sign; unless you are the noodles, reach for 要 yào."},
"noodles|好的！":{"why":"要辣的吗 yào là de ma is a yes-or-no question, and 好的 hǎo de is a cheerful yes, so she'll reach for the chilli oil. In Chinese you answer a yes-no question by echoing its verb: 要 yào for yes, 不要 bú yào for no. Notice 不 changes to bú before the fourth-tone 要, so it's bú yào là, not bù yào là. If you want just a touch of heat, 微辣 wēi là, mildly spicy, is the magic word.","tip":"Answer the verb you heard: she said 要, so say 不要 bú yào."},
"noodles|我不辣。":{"why":"In Chinese, adjectives like 辣 là act as verbs on their own, so 我不辣 wǒ bú là is a complete sentence meaning 'I am not spicy', a comment about you, not the food. She can guess what you mean, but the question was 要辣的吗 yào là de ma, 'want it spicy?', so the clean answer reuses her verb 要: 不要辣 bú yào là. Locals often drop the subject entirely, which makes it sound even more natural.","tip":"Put 要 back in: no 要, no order."},
"noodles|是。":{"why":"A question with 还是 háishi offers a menu of options: A 还是 B, 'A or B?'. 是 shì can't answer that because there's nothing to say yes to, and it also isn't really how Chinese says yes anyway. Just repeat the option you want: 在这儿吃 zài zhèr chī, eat here, or 带走 dàizǒu, take away. Note that 还是 is for questions; in a statement 'or' is usually 或者 huòzhě.","tip":"Hear 还是, pick a side: say the option back, never 'yes'."},
"noodles|给你八十块。":{"why":"Chinese numbers are built like a sum: 十八 shíbā is 10 + 8, while 八十 bāshí is 8 × 10. The rule is simple: if 十 comes first, it's a teen; if a digit comes before 十, you're multiplying. Listen for the first syllable: shí, rising, means the teens, and she started with shí. Most people pay by 扫码 sǎo mǎ (scanning a QR code) anyway, which saves you from counting notes.","tip":"Digit before 十 multiplies, digit after 十 adds."},
"noodles|多少钱？":{"why":"一共 yígòng means 'altogether', and in shops it's almost always followed immediately by the total, so it's a great signal word to latch onto. 多少钱 duōshao qián is perfectly polite, but asking it after she's just said the price makes her repeat herself. Her second question, 扫码还是现金 sǎo mǎ háishi xiànjīn, is another 还是 'or' question, so the best reply is simply one choice: 扫码.","tip":"When you hear yígòng, your ears go on alert: the number comes next."},
"noodles|好，我要牛肉面。":{"why":"卖完了 mài wán le breaks down neatly: 卖 mài is sell, 完 wán means finished, and 了 le marks the change, so 'sold until finished'. Chinese often tacks 完 onto a verb to show something is used up: 吃完了 chī wán le, all eaten. She's also offering 换一个吧 huàn yí ge ba, 'switch to another?', so ordering beef noodles again just loops back. Asking 你推荐什么 nǐ tuījiàn shénme lets her pick, and owners love recommending.","tip":"完 wán = the end; 卖完了 means the shelf is empty."},
"noodles|没问题。":{"why":"没问题 méi wèntí means 'no problem', which accepts her apology nicely, but her real question was 换一个吧 huàn yí ge ba, 'pick something else?'. Without a choice the conversation stalls and she'll just stand there waiting. A good move is to hand the decision back with 那你推荐什么 nà nǐ tuījiàn shénme; the 那 nà, 'then' or 'in that case', smoothly connects to the bad news she just gave.","tip":"After bad news, start with 那 nà, 'then...', and keep the order moving."},
"noodles|五十分钟？太久了！":{"why":"十五 shíwǔ starts with 十 shí, so it's a teen: 10 + 5 = 15. 五十 wǔshí starts with the digit, so it's 5 × 10 = 50. The first syllable tells you everything: rising shí means teens, a third-tone wǔ first means fifties. She also asked 可以吗 kěyǐ ma, 'is that okay?', and the natural yes is to echo it: 可以 kěyǐ.","tip":"First syllable shí? It's a teen. Digit first? It's tens."},
"noodles|没关系。":{"why":"没关系 méi guānxi, 'it doesn't matter', is the standard reply to 对不起 duìbuqǐ or 不好意思 bù hǎoyìsi. She didn't apologise here; she asked permission with 可以吗 kěyǐ ma, 'is that okay?'. Chinese answers a yes-no question by repeating the key word, so 可以 kěyǐ, 'it's okay', is the clean reply, and adding 我等一下 wǒ děng yíxià, 'I'll wait a bit', sounds relaxed.","tip":"Question ends in 可以吗? Answer 可以."},
"noodles|香菜，谢谢。":{"why":"要不要 yào bu yào is the 'want or not want' pattern, and naming an item plus 谢谢 xièxie is exactly how people accept something, so she heard 'coriander please'. To refuse, put 不要 bú yào in front of the item: 不要香菜 bú yào xiāngcài. Coriander is a hot topic in China, loved and hated in equal measure, so this phrase is one of the most used lines in any noodle shop.","tip":"Thanks means yes; to say no, 不要 goes first."},
"noodles|我不香菜。":{"why":"不 bù negates verbs and adjectives, not nouns, so 我不香菜 wǒ bù xiāngcài is 'I not coriander', with nothing being negated. Slot in 要 yào, want, and it becomes 我不要香菜, where 不 also shifts to the second tone because 要 is fourth tone: bú yào. In fast speech locals drop the 我 entirely and just say 不要香菜.","tip":"不 needs a verb to lean on: 不 + 要 + thing."},
"noodles|有，给你十二块。":{"why":"Both numbers start with 十 shí, so they're both teens, and the difference is the last syllable: 八 bā (high, flat) is 8, while 二 èr (falling) is 2. Paying 12 for an 18-kuai bill leaves you 6 short, and she'll have to chase you for it. When you only have a bigger note, say 给你二十 gěi nǐ èrshí and let her give you change; 找钱 zhǎo qián is giving change.","tip":"Bā is high and flat like a level shelf; èr drops like a coin falling."},
"noodles|我是现金。":{"why":"是 shì means 'equals', so 我是现金 wǒ shì xiànjīn claims you are cash. Possession uses 有 yǒu: she asked 有现金吗 yǒu xiànjīn ma, and the Chinese way to say yes is to echo the verb, 有 yǒu. Then just hand it over with 给你二十块 gěi nǐ èrshí kuài.","tip":"She asked with 有, so answer with 有."},
"taxi|我去火车。":{"why":"火车 huǒchē (literally 'fire vehicle', from the steam days) is the train, and 站 zhàn means station or stop, so 火车站 huǒchēzhàn is the train station. The same 站 shows up everywhere: 地铁站 dìtiězhàn metro station, 公交站 gōngjiāozhàn bus stop. Big cities often have several stations, so drivers will ask 哪个火车站 nǎge huǒchēzhàn; it helps to know the name, like 北京南站 Běijīng Nánzhàn.","tip":"No 站, no station: 站 zhàn is where you stand and wait."},
"taxi|火车站在哪儿？":{"why":"火车站在哪儿 huǒchēzhàn zài nǎr is a perfectly good question, but it asks the driver where the station is, as if you want directions. He asked 去哪儿 qù nǎr, 'go where?', so the answer just reuses 去 with the place: 去火车站 qù huǒchēzhàn. Chinese answers often mirror the question's shape, so swap 哪儿 nǎr for your destination and you're done.","tip":"Replace 哪儿 in his question with your place: 去哪儿 becomes 去火车站."},
"taxi|对对对。":{"why":"对 duì means 'correct', so 对对对 is an enthusiastic 'yes, exactly', which here agrees that you're in a hurry and okays the 20-kuai expressway. 赶时间 gǎn shíjiān literally means 'chasing time', and 走高速 zǒu gāosù is taking the expressway. If you're not in a hurry, 不急 bù jí, 'no rush', is a calm, friendly answer, and if you're lost, 什么 shénme or 再说一遍 zài shuō yí biàn, 'say it again', buys you time.","tip":"Never 对对对 to something you didn't understand; buy time with 什么?"},
"taxi|我不赶时间吗。":{"why":"吗 ma is a question particle: stick it on the end of any statement and it becomes a yes-no question. So 我不赶时间吗 means 'am I not in a hurry?', which sounds puzzled rather than informative. Answers just drop the 吗 and keep the rest: 我不赶时间 wǒ bù gǎn shíjiān. Note 不 stays fourth tone bù here because 赶 gǎn is third tone.","tip":"吗 is a question mark you say out loud; leave it off your answers."},
"taxi|我停。":{"why":"With 我 wǒ in front, 停 tíng describes what you yourself will do, 'I stop', which sounds odd from the back seat. Requests to drivers usually skip the subject and name the spot: 前面停一下 qiánmiàn tíng yíxià, 'stop up ahead a moment'. The 一下 yíxià softens it, turning a command into a friendly request, and 这儿停 zhèr tíng, 'stop here', works when you're right there.","tip":"Tell him where, not who: place + 停一下."},
"taxi|十四？给你十四块。":{"why":"Chinese numbers read like arithmetic: 四十二 sìshí'èr is 4 × 10 + 2 = 42, while 十四 shísì is 10 + 4 = 14. The first syllable gives it away: sì, falling sharply, means you're in the forties; shí, rising, means the teens. 四 and 十 are famously hard to tell apart, so if you're unsure, just look at the meter or ask 多少 duōshao again.","tip":"Sì drops like a stone, shí rises like a question."},
"taxi|太贵了，三十吧！":{"why":"Official taxis in mainland China charge by the meter, and the 42 he quoted is what it shows, so bargaining makes no sense and can come across as accusing him of cheating. 打表 dǎbiǎo is the verb for running the meter; if a driver ever refuses to 打表 and names a flat price, that's the moment to negotiate or get out. Most people pay with 扫码 sǎo mǎ, scanning his QR code, or through the ride app itself.","tip":"Meter on, mouth closed: bargain at stalls, not in taxis."},
"taxi|我在中国。":{"why":"哪国人 nǎ guó rén means 'person of which country', so he's asking your nationality, not where you are right now. 在 zài is about location, which he can already see. The pattern is 我是 + country + 人: 我是英国人 wǒ shì Yīngguó rén, I'm British. A thank-you for his compliment is a nice extra: 哪里哪里 nǎlǐ nǎlǐ, 'oh, not at all', is the classic modest reply.","tip":"人 in the question means 人 in your answer: 我是…人."},
"taxi|谢谢，我英国。":{"why":"Leaving out 是 shì and 人 rén makes it sound like 'me Britain', which a driver will understand but is clearly broken. 英国 Yīngguó is the country; add 人 rén, person, to make it a nationality, and 是 shì to link it to you: 我是英国人. Your 谢谢 for the compliment was a nice touch; many Chinese would modestly say 哪里哪里 nǎlǐ nǎlǐ, but thanks is fine and increasingly common.","tip":"Country + 人 = a person from there: 英国人, 美国人, 中国人."},
"taxi|后排？我坐后面。":{"why":"也 yě, also, is the key word: 后排也要系安全带 hòupái yě yào jì ānquándài means 'the back row also has to buckle up'. He isn't asking where you're sitting; he's reminding you of the rule, which is enforced in many cities now. Note that 系 here is pronounced jì, the everyday word for tying or fastening, and 系上 jì shàng means 'buckle it on'.","tip":"Hear 也 yě and think 'too': the rule covers you as well."},
"taxi|我很安全。":{"why":"Chinese builds lots of words by stacking meanings: 安全 ānquán, safe, plus 带 dài, belt or strap, gives 安全带 ānquándài, the seatbelt, literally a 'safety strap'. He's asking you to fasten it, so 我很安全 wǒ hěn ānquán, 'I'm very safe', sounds like you're refusing. The verb for buckling up is 系 jì: 好，我系上 hǎo, wǒ jì shàng.","tip":"安全 + 带 = safety + strap = seatbelt."},
"taxi|好的。":{"why":"开窗还是开空调 kāi chuāng háishi kāi kōngtiáo offers two options joined by 还是 háishi, 'or', so 好的 hǎo de, 'okay', leaves him guessing which one. Pick one and repeat it back. Adding 吧 ba at the end, as in 开空调吧 kāi kōngtiáo ba, makes it a relaxed suggestion rather than an order.","tip":"还是 = or; answer with a choice, not a yes."},
"taxi|开空气吧。":{"why":"Both words start with 空 kōng, empty or air, so they're easy to mix up. 空气 kōngqì is the air you breathe, while 空调 kōngtiáo is short for 空气调节 kōngqì tiáojié, 'air adjusting', the air-conditioner. The driver will probably guess what you mean, but 开空气 sounds like 'turn on the air' in a strange, literal way.","tip":"调 tiáo means adjust: the machine adjusts the air, so it's 空调."},
"taxi|我不换车。":{"why":"换 huàn means change or switch, and 条 tiáo is the measure word for long, thin things like roads and rivers, so 换条路 huàn tiáo lù is 'switch to another road'. 堵车 dǔ chē means traffic jam, literally 'blocked cars'. Replying 我不换车 sounds like you think he's asking you to change taxis. Locals let the driver choose with 行，听你的 xíng, tīng nǐ de, 'sure, your call', which drivers appreciate.","tip":"条 tiáo is for long skinny things, and roads are long and skinny."},
"taxi|不行，快一点！":{"why":"不行 bù xíng is a flat 'no way', and 快一点 kuài yìdiǎn as a bare command sounds impatient, especially since he's trying to help you get around a jam. Taxi drivers know the roads far better than any app, and agreeing to a detour usually saves time. 行 xíng, 'okay', or 听你的 tīng nǐ de, 'I'll listen to you', keeps things friendly; if you really are in a rush, 我有点儿急 wǒ yǒudiǎnr jí, 'I'm in a bit of a hurry', explains without bossing.","tip":"Let the driver drive: 听你的, 'your call'."},
"market|便宜！":{"why":"Said on its own, 便宜 piányi is an exclamation, 'cheap!', which tells the seller you think 150 is a bargain, the opposite of what you want. Adding 一点儿 yìdiǎnr, 'a little', turns it into a comparison, 'a bit cheaper', and 吧 ba at the end makes it a coaxing request: 便宜一点儿吧. Mind the tones: 便宜 is pián plus a light, neutral yi; the same character 便 is biàn in 方便 fāngbiàn, convenient.","tip":"Cheap alone is praise; cheap plus 一点儿 is a haggle."},
"market|好，我买。":{"why":"At night markets and tourist stalls the first price is just an opening move, and sellers fully expect a counter-offer, so saying 好，我买 hǎo, wǒ mǎi straight away means you'll pay well over the going rate. Starting with 太贵了 tài guì le, 'too expensive', signals you know the game and invites her to ask 你说多少 nǐ shuō duōshao, 'what's your price?'. Bargaining is friendly theatre here, so smile while you do it. In malls and chain stores prices are fixed, so this only applies to markets.","tip":"First price is a starting gun, not a price tag: open with 太贵了."},
"market|一百四。":{"why":"一百四 yìbǎi sì is shorthand for 一百四十 yìbǎi sìshí, 140; when a round number follows 百, Chinese drops the final 十. Offering 140 against 150 barely moves her, and if she grabs it you've overpaid. A common market strategy is to open at around a third of the asking price, so 四十块 sìshí kuài, and meet somewhere in the middle. Watch the tone change: 一 becomes yì before the third-tone 百 bǎi.","tip":"Open low, around a third, so you have room to climb."},
"market|四块。":{"why":"四块 sì kuài is just 4 kuai, which sounds like a joke for a T-shirt, so she won't bargain seriously from there. The number you wanted is 四十 sìshí, 4 × 10 = 40. Chinese prices use 块 kuài in speech instead of the written 元 yuán, and the 十 matters a lot: 四块 4, 十四块 14, 四十块 40.","tip":"Check for the 十 shí: without it you're offering pocket change."},
"market|好吧，一百。":{"why":"不行不行 bù xíng bù xíng, 'no way, no way', is part of the routine, not a final answer. 好吧 hǎo ba is a reluctant 'fine then', and accepting 100 means you jumped from 40 to her number in one go. The strongest move at a market is to walk away politely: 那算了 nà suàn le means 'forget it then', and 我不要了 wǒ bú yào le, 'I don't want it anymore'. Very often the seller calls you back with a lower price.","tip":"Her 不行 is a move in the game; your walk-away is a better one."},
"market|好，我买二件。":{"why":"Chinese has two words for 'two'. 二 èr is the digit, used in counting, phone numbers, and inside bigger numbers like 十二 shí'èr. When you count things with a measure word, you use 两 liǎng: 两件 liǎng jiàn, two tops, since 件 jiàn is the measure word for shirts and jackets. She'll understand 二件, but it sounds like a beginner.","tip":"Counting things with a measure word? Two is 两 liǎng."},
"market|三个。":{"why":"In Chinese conversation, repeating a key word back is how you agree, so saying 三个 sān ge sounds like 'yes, three'. 几个 jǐ ge asks how many, so give your own number. 一个就够了 yí ge jiù gòu le means 'one is enough': 就 jiù adds 'just', and 够了 gòu le is 'enough'. It's a polite, firm way to turn down a bulk deal.","tip":"Echo means yes; say your own number to say no."},
"market|没关系。":{"why":"没关系 méi guānxi means 'it doesn't matter' or 'no worries', the reply to someone else's apology. Here she scolded you with 别拍照 bié pāizhào, 'don't take photos', so 没关系 sounds like you're forgiving her for telling you off. The right move is a quick apology: 不好意思 bù hǎoyìsi, then 不拍了 bù pāi le, 'I'll stop'. The 了 le there signals a change, I was doing it, now I'm not.","tip":"Who's sorry? If it's you, say 不好意思; if it's them, say 没关系."},
"market|我要拍照。":{"why":"别 bié is the 'don't' of commands, a short form of 不要 bú yào, so 别拍照 is a clear 'no photos'. Many sellers worry about people copying designs or posting prices online. Answering 我要拍照 wǒ yào pāizhào, 'I want to take a photo', is a direct refusal, and it sours the mood before you've even started bargaining. A quick 不好意思，不拍了 bù hǎoyìsi, bù pāi le smooths it over.","tip":"别 bié = don't; when you hear it, stop and apologise."},
"market|我要大号。":{"why":"没有大号了 méiyǒu dà hào le means 'no more large ones', the 了 le marking that they've just run out. 大号 dà hào is size large, alongside 中号 zhōng hào medium and 小号 xiǎo hào small. Insisting 我要大号 doesn't help because there aren't any in that colour; she's offering 黑色的 hēisè de, the black one. 的 de here stands in for the noun, so 黑色的 means 'the black one'.","tip":"没有…了 means sold out, so look at what she offers instead."},
"market|白色的行。":{"why":"黑 hēi and 白 bái are both common, so they're easy to swap in the moment. Colours add 色 sè, colour, to the base: 黑色 hēisè black, 白色 báisè white, 红色 hóngsè red. Adding 也 yě, as in 黑色的也行 hēisè de yě xíng, 'black's fine too', sounds flexible and friendly.","tip":"白 bái has the sun 日 inside it, so it is the bright one; 黑 hēi is the dark one."},
"market|下次？我下次不来。":{"why":"下次 xià cì means next time; 下 xià is 'next' or 'down', and 次 cì counts occurrences. Adding sellers on 微信 Wēixìn, WeChat, is a normal way to keep regular customers, and 我下次不来 wǒ xià cì bù lái, 'I won't come next time', sounds bluntly rude. To accept, say 好啊，我扫你 hǎo a, wǒ sǎo nǐ, 'sure, I'll scan you'. To decline kindly, 不用了，谢谢 bú yòng le, xièxie softens it.","tip":"下次 is next time; turn things down with 不用了, never 不来."},
"directions|喂！地铁在哪儿？":{"why":"喂 wèi is the standard 'hello' when you pick up the phone, but shouted at someone face to face it's like yelling 'hey you!', and strangers may ignore you. The polite opener is 你好 nǐ hǎo or 不好意思 bù hǎoyìsi, then 请问 qǐngwèn, literally 'may I ask', which signals a question is coming. Also note 地铁 dìtiě is the metro system; the station is 地铁站 dìtiězhàn.","tip":"喂 is for phones; for people, it's 请问 qǐngwèn."},
"directions|你好吗？地铁站？":{"why":"你好吗 nǐ hǎo ma is how textbooks teach 'how are you?', but in real life it's for people you know, and from a stranger it sounds oddly personal. Chinese greetings tend to skip wellbeing questions; 你好 nǐ hǎo is just 'hello', and 请问 qǐngwèn, 'may I ask', leads into your question. Note the tone sandhi: 你好 is pronounced ní hǎo because two third tones in a row turn the first one into a rising tone.","tip":"Strangers get 请问, friends get 你好吗."},
"directions|往右拐，对吗？":{"why":"往 wǎng means 'towards', and 拐 guǎi is turn, so 往左拐 wǎng zuǒ guǎi is 'turn towards the left'. 左 zuǒ and 右 yòu look fairly alike, and you'll hear them a lot. The character trick: 左 has 工 gōng, work, and 右 has 口 kǒu, mouth. Most people eat with their right hand, and the mouth is in 右.","tip":"The mouth 口 is in 右: right hand feeds the mouth."},
"directions|五分钟？很远吗？":{"why":"大概五分钟 dàgài wǔ fēnzhōng already told you it's about five minutes' walk, so 很远吗 hěn yuǎn ma, 'is it far?', asks something she just answered. The useful thing to check is the turn, because that's where people get lost. Repeating the instruction back with 对吗 duì ma, 'right?', is a clear, polite way to confirm: 往左拐，对吗.","tip":"Confirm the turn, not the time: repeat the direction plus 对吗."},
"directions|两号线。":{"why":"两 liǎng counts quantities with a measure word: 两张 liǎng zhāng, two tickets. But 号 hào turns a number into a label, Line 2, Room 2, the 2nd, and labels always use the digit 二 èr: 二号线 èr hào xiàn. People will understand 两号线, but it sounds like 'line a-couple'. The same goes for dates, like 二月二号 èr yuè èr hào, 2 February.","tip":"Counting things: 两. Naming things with 号: 二."},
"directions|我坐地铁。":{"why":"几 jǐ asks for a number, usually a small one, and 号线 hào xiàn means 'numbered line', so 几号线 is 'line number what?'. You'd already told her you're going to the metro station, so she wants the line, not the transport. 坐 zuò, sit, is the verb for riding public transport, as in 坐地铁 zuò dìtiě, so 坐二号线 zuò èr hào xiàn works too.","tip":"Hear 几 jǐ, answer with a number."},
"directions|不客气！":{"why":"不客气 bú kèqi means 'you're welcome', literally 'don't be so polite', so it's what the helper says, not the person being helped. Saying it to her flips the roles. The thanks is 谢谢 xièxie or 谢谢你 xièxie nǐ; 谢谢 itself takes a light neutral tone on the second syllable. If she then says 不客气 to you, the exchange is perfect.","tip":"Receiving help: 谢谢. Giving help: 不客气."},
"directions|对不起，对不起。":{"why":"对不起 duìbuqǐ literally means 'I can't face you', so it's for genuine wrongdoing, like bumping into someone or breaking something. For the small inconvenience of asking a stranger, 不好意思 bù hǎoyìsi, 'I'm embarrassed', is the lighter choice, and once she's helped you, 谢谢 xièxie is the natural ending. A double 对不起 sounds like you've done something quite serious.","tip":"对不起 for mistakes, 不好意思 for bother, 谢谢 for help."},
"directions|你是哪国人？":{"why":"本地人 běndì rén means 'a local', literally 'person of this place', and China has a huge internal migration, so many people in a big city came from another province. She's saying she's not from this city, which is why she doesn't know the way. Asking 你是哪国人 nǐ shì nǎ guó rén, 'which country are you from?', suggests she's a foreigner, which sounds odd. A simple 好的，谢谢 hǎo de, xièxie lets you move on.","tip":"本地 = this place; 外地人 wàidì rén is someone from elsewhere in China."},
"directions|对不起。":{"why":"She's the one who couldn't help, so there's nothing for you to be sorry about, and 对不起 duìbuqǐ is a heavy apology for wrongdoing. Thanking someone for trying is common in Chinese too: 好的，谢谢 hǎo de, xièxie, 'okay, thanks'. If you want to soften it, 没事 méi shì, 'no worries', also fits nicely.","tip":"When someone tries to help, thank them, even if it didn't work."},
"directions|有。":{"why":"Echoing the verb 有 yǒu is exactly how Chinese says yes, so she now thinks you can scan in. 乘车码 chéngchē mǎ is the 'ride code' in apps like Alipay or WeChat that lets you scan through the gates. With a flat battery, say 我手机没电了 wǒ shǒujī méi diàn le, literally 'my phone has no electricity', and she'll point you to the ticket machines.","tip":"Echo the verb only when the answer is truly yes."},
"directions|我手机死了。":{"why":"死 sǐ is literally 'die', used for living things, and Chinese doesn't stretch it to gadgets the way English does. The everyday phrase is 没电了 méi diàn le, 'out of electricity'. Chinese also uses 死 in phrases like 累死了 lèi sǐ le, 'dead tired', but a phone with 死了 sounds odd. If it's actually broken, 坏了 huài le, broken, is the word.","tip":"Phones run out of 电 diàn, they don't die."},
"directions|红灯？往左拐？":{"why":"小心 xiǎoxīn, literally 'small heart', means be careful, and when shouted it's a warning to stop right now. 红灯 hóngdēng is the red light and 等一下 děng yíxià is 'wait a moment', so she's saving you from traffic, not giving directions. A warm thanks is 谢谢提醒 xièxie tíxǐng, 'thanks for the reminder'. 提醒 tíxǐng means remind or warn.","tip":"小心 = freeze; think first, then thank."},
"directions|没事，没有车。":{"why":"没事 méi shì, 'it's fine', brushes off her warning, which can come across as dismissive when she's looking out for you. Many Chinese cities now have cameras at crossings, some even put jaywalkers' faces on a screen, and police can fine you. Wait for 绿灯 lǜdēng, the green light, and thank her with 谢谢提醒 xièxie tíxǐng. Note 绿 lǜ has the ü vowel, like French 'tu'.","tip":"Red 红 hóng means wait, green 绿 lǜ means go."},
"directions|好，谢谢你麻烦。":{"why":"麻烦 máfan means trouble or bother, and the set phrase is 麻烦你了 máfan nǐ le, 'I've troubled you', a warm thank-you for someone going out of their way. 谢谢你麻烦 jumbles two phrases together. You'll also hear 麻烦你 at the start of requests, like 麻烦你帮我… máfan nǐ bāng wǒ, 'could I trouble you to help me…'. Saying 太好了 tài hǎo le first, 'great!', shows you're happy to accept.","tip":"Someone goes out of their way for you? 麻烦你了."},
"directions|不要，我自己走。":{"why":"不要 bú yào means 'don't want', which sounds like a flat rejection of her kindness. 我自己走 wǒ zìjǐ zǒu, 'I'll walk myself', makes it even colder. Offering to walk with a lost visitor is a generous gesture in China, and accepting with 太好了，麻烦你了 tài hǎo le, máfan nǐ le is gracious. If you'd rather not, 不用了，谢谢 bú yòng le, xièxie, 'no need, thanks', is the polite way to decline.","tip":"不要 refuses the thing; 不用了 politely declines the favour."},
"pharmacy|我有头。":{"why":"有 yǒu is about possessing or existing, so 我有头 wǒ yǒu tóu states, quite truthfully, that you own a head. Symptoms in Chinese follow the pattern body part + how it feels: 头疼 tóu téng, head hurts; 肚子疼 dùzi téng, stomach hurts; 嗓子疼 sǎngzi téng, sore throat. 哪里不舒服 nǎlǐ bù shūfu literally asks 'where is uncomfortable?', so naming the place plus 疼 fits perfectly.","tip":"Where + 疼 téng: point to the part, then say it hurts."},
"pharmacy|我疼头。":{"why":"In Chinese, 疼 téng behaves like an adjective describing the body part, so the part is the topic and 疼 comes after it: 头疼 tóu téng, 'head is sore'. 疼头 téng tóu puts it in verb-object order, like 'hurt the head', which sounds like you're hurting something. You'll also hear 头痛 tóu tòng, which means the same thing; 头疼 is more conversational.","tip":"Topic first, feeling second: 头 then 疼."},
"pharmacy|二天，没有发烧。":{"why":"When you count units like days, people or items, two is 两 liǎng: 两天 liǎng tiān, 两个人 liǎng ge rén. 二 èr is the pure digit, used for counting out loud, in numbers like 十二 shí'èr, and in labels like 二号 èr hào. Adding 了 le, as in 两天了 liǎng tiān le, means 'for two days now', the natural way to say how long something has lasted.","tip":"Two days, two people, two bowls: always 两 liǎng."},
"pharmacy|很久。":{"why":"很久 hěn jiǔ, 'a long time', is vague, and a pharmacist needs to know whether it's hours, days or weeks to decide what to give you. 多久 duō jiǔ asks for a duration, so answer with a number and a time word: 两天 liǎng tiān, 三个小时 sān ge xiǎoshí, 一个星期 yí ge xīngqī. Note 很久 is pronounced hén jiǔ because two third tones in a row make the first one rise.","tip":"多久 wants a number; give it a number plus 天, 小时 or 星期."},
"pharmacy|一天两次，一次三片？":{"why":"Dosage in Chinese is a neat formula: 一天 yì tiān + number + 次 cì means times per day, and 一次 yí cì + number + 片 piàn means tablets per time. She said 三次 sān cì and 两片 liǎng piàn, and you swapped them, which would change how much medicine you take. 片 piàn is the measure word for flat things like tablets, slices and leaves.","tip":"次 is how often, 片 is how many pills: frequency then dose."},
"pharmacy|饭前吃，对吧？":{"why":"前 qián means before or in front, and 后 hòu means after or behind; they pair with time words everywhere: 饭前 fàn qián before meals, 饭后 fàn hòu after meals, 以前 yǐqián before, 以后 yǐhòu later. Some medicines must go on a full stomach, so this distinction really matters. Checking with 对吧 duì ba was a smart move, you just had the word reversed.","tip":"前 qián is in front, 后 hòu is behind: 饭后 means the meal comes first, then the pill."},
"pharmacy|过敏。":{"why":"In Chinese, echoing the key word from a yes-no question is how you say yes, so 过敏 guòmǐn on its own means 'yes, allergic'. To say no, negate it with 不: 不过敏 bú guòmǐn, where 不 rises to bú before the fourth-tone 过. If you do have an allergy, name it with 对 duì: 我对青霉素过敏 wǒ duì qīngméisù guòmǐn, 'I'm allergic to penicillin'.","tip":"Echo = yes; 不 + echo = no."},
"pharmacy|不，我没睡好。":{"why":"English 'no' agrees with the fact (I didn't sleep well), but Chinese 不 bù or 不是 bú shì responds to the speaker's statement, so 不 means 'you're wrong'. She guessed 你昨天没睡好吧 nǐ zuótiān méi shuì hǎo ba, 'you didn't sleep well, right?', so if she's right you say 对 duì, 'correct'. Then repeat the fact: 对，没睡好 duì, méi shuì hǎo.","tip":"In Chinese you're judging her guess: right is 对, wrong is 不."},
"pharmacy|我的脸没问题。":{"why":"脸 liǎn is face and 色 sè is colour, so 脸色 liǎnsè is your complexion, and 脸色不太好 liǎnsè bú tài hǎo is the everyday way to say someone looks pale or unwell. Chinese people comment on this out of concern, not rudeness, and it often leads to a question about sleep or health. Answering that your face is fine misses the point; agree with 对，没睡好 duì, méi shuì hǎo.","tip":"脸色 is the colour of your face, a health report, not a beauty comment."},
"pharmacy|我要布洛芬。":{"why":"没有了 méiyǒu le means they had some but now there's none, so asking for 布洛芬 bùluòfēn again won't help. She's offering a substitute that's 效果一样 xiàoguǒ yíyàng, 'the same effect', and 还便宜 hái piányi, 'cheaper as well'. A simple 那就要这个 nà jiù yào zhège, 'then I'll take this one', accepts it; 那 nà links to her news and 就 jiù means 'just' or 'then'.","tip":"没有了 closes the door; 那就 nà jiù walks through the next one."},
"pharmacy|贵一点儿吗？":{"why":"还 hái here means 'also' or 'on top of that', so 还便宜 hái piányi adds a bonus: it works the same and it's cheaper. 贵 guì means expensive, the opposite of 便宜 piányi, so you asked about the exact opposite of what she said. Listen for 还 as a stacking word; it often introduces an extra selling point.","tip":"还 hái adds a bonus: 'and also'."},
"pharmacy|我没有医生。":{"why":"医 yī means medicine or healing, and it starts many health words: 医生 yīshēng doctor, 医院 yīyuàn hospital, 医保 yībǎo medical insurance, short for 医疗保险 yīliáo bǎoxiǎn. 医保卡 yībǎo kǎ is the national health insurance card residents use to pay at pharmacies. As a visitor you won't have one, so say 没有，我自己付 méiyǒu, wǒ zìjǐ fù, 'no, I'll pay myself'.","tip":"医 = medicine; 保 bǎo = protect; 医保 is medical protection, insurance."},
"pharmacy|我不有。":{"why":"Chinese has two negatives: 不 bù for most verbs and adjectives, and 没 méi for 有 yǒu and completed actions. 有 is the special case that never takes 不, so it's always 没有 méiyǒu, and 不有 sounds instantly wrong to a native ear. In quick replies, 没有 on its own is a complete 'no, I don't'.","tip":"有 only dates 没: it's always 没有."},
"store|再要一个水。":{"why":"瓶 píng means bottle, and it's the measure word for anything that comes in one: 一瓶水 yì píng shuǐ, 一瓶啤酒 yì píng píjiǔ. 个 ge is the catch-all, and plenty of people do say 一个水 in a shop, but 瓶 shows you know the word. 再要 zài yào means 'also want' or 'one more thing'. Note 一 is yì before second-tone 瓶 but yí before 个.","tip":"Bottle-shaped? Say 瓶 píng."},
"store|没有。":{"why":"就这些吗 jiù zhèxiē ma asks 'is that all?', and 没有 méiyǒu works as 'there isn't any' or 'don't have', so it sounds like 'nothing else', the opposite of what you want. Chinese adds extra items with 再 zài or 还 hái: 再要一瓶水 zài yào yì píng shuǐ or 还要一瓶水 hái yào yì píng shuǐ, 'I also want a bottle of water'. If you are done, you'd say 对，就这些 duì, jiù zhèxiē.","tip":"More stuff? Start with 还 or 再."},
"store|不要。":{"why":"不要 bú yào is grammatically fine, but it's a flat 'don't want', which can sound curt to someone offering you something. 不用了 bú yòng le means 'no need', implying you appreciate the offer but don't need it; the 了 le softens it further. Adding 谢谢 xièxie makes it perfectly polite. Bags cost a few mao in China, so clerks always ask.","tip":"Turning down an offer? 不用了, never just 不要."},
"store|要。|要 yào means yes,":{"why":"In Chinese you say yes by echoing the question's verb, so 要 yào after 要袋子吗 yào dàizi ma is a clear 'yes, I want a bag'. Plastic bags aren't free in China; supermarkets and convenience stores charge a small fee, so you'll pay for it too. To decline, say 不用了 bú yòng le, 'no need'.","tip":"Echo means yes: only say 要 if you want it."},
"store|热。":{"why":"Chinese does say yes by echoing a verb, so a clerk may well take a bare 热 rè as 'heat it'. The trouble is 热 is also the adjective 'hot', as in 我很热 wǒ hěn rè, 'I'm hot', so on its own it's ambiguous and sounds clipped. The main verb in her question is 要 yào, so the cleanest yes is 要, and adding 热一下吧 rè yíxià ba, 'heat it up a bit', confirms what you want.","tip":"Answer with the first verb of the question: 要."},
"store|是，热。":{"why":"Chinese has no single word for 'yes'; you echo the verb of the question. 是 shì only answers questions built with 是, like 你是学生吗 nǐ shì xuésheng ma. She asked 要热一下吗 yào rè yíxià ma, so the verb is 要 yào. 是，热 sounds like 'correct, hot', which she'll understand but sounds robotic.","tip":"The question's verb is your yes: 要吗? 要."},
"store|十二块？给你。":{"why":"Chinese money has three units: 块 kuài (yuan), 毛 máo (a tenth of a yuan) and 分 fēn (a hundredth). In speech, a number after 块 means 毛, so 十二块五 shí'èr kuài wǔ is 12 yuan and 5 mao, 12.50. People drop the final 毛 the same way English drops 'cents' in 'twelve fifty'. If you only hand over 12, you're 5 mao short.","tip":"Number after 块? That's tenths: 块五 = .50."},
"store|二十五块？":{"why":"The order of syllables builds the number: 十二 shí'èr is 10 + 2 = 12, and 二十五 èrshíwǔ is 2 × 10 + 5 = 25. She said 十二块五, so the 五 at the end is the mao, 12.50, not part of a bigger number. The first syllable is your clue: shí first means the teens.","tip":"Shí first means teens; digit first means tens."},
"store|有！":{"why":"会员 huìyuán means member, and chains like FamilyMart or Lawson have loyalty programmes where you give your phone number for points. Echoing 有 yǒu says yes, so the clerk will ask for your 手机号 shǒujī hào, phone number. The negative of 有 is always 没有 méiyǒu.","tip":"Not a member? 没有, and you're out of the loyalty loop."},
"store|不是。":{"why":"Chinese answers mirror the verb in the question. She asked 你有会员吗 nǐ yǒu huìyuán ma, literally 'do you have membership?', so the answer is 有 yǒu or 没有 méiyǒu. 不是 bú shì would fit 你是会员吗 nǐ shì huìyuán ma, 'are you a member?', which people also say, so she understood you. Matching her verb sounds smoother.","tip":"Listen for the verb in her question and reuse it."},
"store|要。|还是 háishi means ":{"why":"冰的还是常温的 bīng de háishi chángwēn de offers two choices, iced or room temperature, so 要 yào alone doesn't pick one. Many Chinese people actually prefer room-temperature water for health reasons, so clerks really do ask. 的 de stands in for the noun, so 冰的 bīng de means 'the cold one'; 要冰的 yào bīng de is a complete, natural answer.","tip":"还是 = or: always answer with your pick."},
"store|要病的。":{"why":"冰 bīng and 病 bìng share the same syllable and differ only in tone: first tone stays high and level, fourth tone drops sharply. 冰的 bīng de is 'the cold one', while 病的 bìng de is 'the sick one', which will get a confused smile. Try saying bīng like a held musical note, then compare it with a short, falling bìng.","tip":"Ice is flat and smooth, bīng; sickness knocks you down, bìng."},
"store|好，热一下吧。":{"why":"Verb + 不了 bu liǎo means 'can't do', usually because circumstances stop it: 热不了 rè bu liǎo can't heat, 吃不了 chī bu liǎo can't finish eating. Here 了 is pronounced liǎo, not le. 坏了 huài le means broken, so the microwave is out of action. A relaxed reply is 没关系，不用热了 méi guānxi, bú yòng rè le, 'no worries, no need to heat it'.","tip":"Verb + 不了 = 'no can do'."},
"store|不客气。":{"why":"The clerk opened with 不好意思 bù hǎoyìsi, which is a small apology, so the natural reply is one that forgives: 没关系 méi guānxi, literally \"there's no connection\", meaning no harm done. 不客气 bú kèqi literally means \"don't be polite with me\", so it only fits after someone thanks you. To a local it sounds like you heard a thank-you that never came, which is a bit puzzling but harmless.","tip":"Sorry gets 没关系, thanks gets 不客气: match the reply to what they just gave you."},
"store|我不带。":{"why":"Chinese marks negation by meaning, not tense: 不 bù negates what you will do, want to do or habitually do, so 我不带 wǒ bú dài sounds like a refusal, \"I'm not taking one\". 没 méi negates something that didn't happen, so 没带 méi dài is \"I didn't bring one\". Notice too that 不 changes to bú before a fourth tone like 带 dài. Her question ended in 了 le, a past-event question, and 没 is how you answer that in the negative.","tip":"If the question has 了, the no-answer uses 没 and drops the 了."},
"store|没带。有三卖吗？":{"why":"伞 sǎn and 三 sān share the exact same sounds, so the tone is the only thing separating umbrella from three. The third tone dips low and, said on its own, rises back up, while the first tone stays high and flat like a held note. A clerk will usually guess umbrella from the rain, but on its own 有三卖吗 yǒu sān mài ma sounds like \"do you sell three?\"","tip":"Picture an umbrella dipping down to shake off rain: 伞 sǎn dips, 三 sān stays flat."},
"metro|机器不好。":{"why":"好 hǎo is about quality or how you feel about something, so 机器不好 jīqì bù hǎo sounds like a judgment, \"this machine is bad\", as if it is slow or badly designed. 坏了 huài le is the go-to phrase for anything that has stopped working, and the 了 le signals a change: it used to work, now it doesn't. Adding 吗 ma turns it into the question you wanted: 坏了吗 huài le ma.","tip":"Broken things in China are always 坏了 huài le, from phones to lifts to ticket machines."},
"metro|我坏了。":{"why":"Chinese puts the thing being described right at the front, so whatever comes before 坏了 huài le is what's broken. 我坏了 wǒ huài le says you have broken down, and 坏 huài on a person can even suggest being bad or naughty. Name the machine first, 这个机器 zhège jīqì, then add 坏了吗 huài le ma.","tip":"Whatever sits in front of 坏了 is the thing that's broken, so put the machine there, not 我."},
"metro|买一个票，去东站。":{"why":"Chinese needs a measure word between a number and a noun, and each one hints at the object's shape. 张 zhāng is for flat, sheet-like things such as tickets, paper, tables and beds, so tickets get 一张票 yì zhāng piào. 个 ge is the all-purpose fallback, so the staff will understand you, but 一张 sounds like you know what you're asking for. Notice 一 yī becomes yì before first-tone 张.","tip":"张 zhāng is for anything flat you could slide across a counter: tickets, cards, paper."},
"metro|我从东站。":{"why":"从 cóng marks a starting point and usually pairs with a destination, as in 从东站到西站 cóng Dōngzhàn dào Xīzhàn. Said alone, 我从东站 wǒ cóng Dōngzhàn sounds unfinished, like \"I, from East Station...\" with the verb missing. She asked 去哪儿 qù nǎr, so the cleanest answer recycles her verb: 去东站 qù Dōngzhàn.","tip":"Answer with the verb in the question: she asked 去哪儿, you say 去 + place."},
"metro|三站以后上车？":{"why":"上 shàng (up) and 下 xià (down) do double duty in Chinese, describing getting on and off vehicles: 上车 shàng chē, 下车 xià chē, 上飞机 shàng fēijī. She said 第三站下 dì sān zhàn xià, \"get off at the third stop\", with 第 dì turning 三 into an ordinal. Your version swaps both the action and the timing, so it describes a different trip.","tip":"You step up into a train with 上 and down out of it with 下."},
"metro|坐三号线？":{"why":"When a sentence has two numbers, the word after each one tells you what it counts: 二号线 èr hào xiàn is Line 2, while 第三站 dì sān zhàn is the third stop. 号 hào marks a number used as a name, like a line, room or date, and 第 dì marks order. Repeating it back is a great habit, just pair each number with its own word.","tip":"号 hào names it, 第 dì orders it: Line 2, third stop."},
"metro|不用了，谢谢。":{"why":"不用了 bú yòng le means \"no need\", which only makes sense when someone offers you something optional. 包放上来 bāo fàng shànglái is an instruction, and metro security checks in mainland China are mandatory for every bag. A cheerful 好的 hǎo de while you put the bag on the belt is all the officer is waiting for.","tip":"Instructions get 好的, offers get 不用了."},
"metro|我没有钱。|She asked about ":{"why":"The subject of her question was 你的卡 nǐ de kǎ, your card, and 是不是 shì bu shì just checks whether her guess is right. 没钱了 méi qián le is the everyday way to say a transit card or phone balance is empty. 我没有钱 wǒ méiyǒu qián shifts the topic to you, and sounds like you're saying you're broke.","tip":"Keep the same subject she used: she asked about the 卡, so answer about the 卡."},
"metro|是不是。":{"why":"A pattern like 是不是 shì bu shì or 对不对 duì bu duì is the question mark itself, not an answer. Repeating it back sounds like you're echoing her, a bit like replying \"is it or isn't it\". Pick one side: 是 shì, 不是 bú shì, or if you honestly don't know, 不知道 bù zhīdào.","tip":"In an A-not-A question, your answer is either the A or the not-A, never both."},
"metro|有，给你四十。":{"why":"Chinese money uses 块 kuài in speech for yuan, and 四块 sì kuài means four of them, no tens anywhere. 四十 sìshí needs that 十 shí, ten, in the middle. 四 sì and 十 shí are easy to blur because of the s and sh sounds, so listen for whether a second syllable follows. She asked for 零钱 língqián, small change, and with only a 50 you'd say 只有五十的 zhǐ yǒu wǔshí de.","tip":"No 十 shí, no tens: 四块 is just four."},
"metro|我没有钱。|没有钱 means you're":{"why":"零钱 língqián means small change, coins or small notes, from 零 líng, zero or bits and pieces. 我没有钱 wǒ méiyǒu qián says you have no money at all, which would leave her wondering how you'll pay. Say 没有零钱 méiyǒu língqián and then mention what you do have.","tip":"零钱 líng qián is money in little bits, so 没有零钱 means no small change, not no money."},
"metro|好吃，你要吗？":{"why":"能 néng is about whether something is possible or allowed, so 不能吃 bù néng chī means eating isn't permitted here. Most mainland metros ban eating on trains, and staff do enforce it. Offering her some sounds like you missed the point, or worse, like you're teasing her.","tip":"能 néng is about permission, so 不能 is a no-entry sign for the verb that follows."},
"metro|我很快吃完。":{"why":"不能 bù néng states a rule, and 啊 a at the end softens her tone but doesn't make it optional. Bargaining for time, 我很快吃完 wǒ hěn kuài chī wán, can sound like you're dismissing her. The smooth move is 不好意思 bù hǎoyìsi plus 我不吃了 wǒ bù chī le, where 了 le signals you're stopping now.","tip":"When staff cite a rule, 不好意思 plus doing it beats any argument."},
"hotel|对，我有房间。":{"why":"有 yǒu means to have or possess, so 我有房间 wǒ yǒu fángjiān sounds like you own a room somewhere, not that you reserved one. 订 dìng means to book or reserve and covers hotels, tables and tickets, and the 了 le marks that it's done. 我订了房间 wǒ dìng le fángjiān tells the desk to look up your booking.","tip":"订 dìng is the booking verb: rooms, tables, tickets, all 订."},
"hotel|我入住你。":{"why":"入住 rùzhù is \"to move in and stay\", and it describes what you do, so it never takes a person as its object. 我入住你 wǒ rùzhù nǐ sounds like you're checking into her, which is confusing and a bit funny. In 办入住 bàn rùzhù the desk handles the paperwork; you just confirm with 对 duì and mention your booking.","tip":"You 入住 the hotel, the desk 办 the check-in; nobody 入住 a person."},
"hotel|我不看。":{"why":"给我看 gěi wǒ kàn literally means \"give me a look\", with 我 wǒ, me, being the one who looks. The 一下 yíxià softens the request to \"just a quick look\". 我不看 wǒ bú kàn means you refuse to look, which doesn't fit since she's the one who wants to see. Handing it over with 给您 gěi nín is the polite reply.","tip":"In 给我看, the 我 is the person doing the looking, and that's her."},
"hotel|照片？":{"why":"Both words share 照 zhào, which relates to taking pictures or official papers: 护照 hùzhào is literally \"protect-permit\", a passport, while 照片 zhàopiàn is a \"picture-slice\", a photo. At a hotel desk, they almost always want 护照 for real-name registration, which is legally required. Checking was smart, just add the right word.","tip":"护 hù means protect, and your passport protects you abroad."},
"hotel|Wi-Fi密码是什么？":{"why":"In Chinese, 多少 duōshao asks for a quantity or a number, and Chinese people think of number codes as a kind of amount, so phone numbers, room numbers and passwords all get 是多少 shì duōshao. 是什么 shì shénme isn't wrong, especially for passwords with letters, but 多少 sounds like a local. The reply will often be just a string of digits.","tip":"Phone number, room number, Wi-Fi code: if it's digits, ask 多少."},
"hotel|我的密码是多少？":{"why":"我的 wǒ de puts the password in your possession, so 我的密码是多少 wǒ de mìmǎ shì duōshao sounds like you forgot your own password and want her to tell you. Leaving out the possessive and naming the thing, Wi-Fi密码 Wi-Fi mìmǎ, makes it clear you mean the hotel's network.","tip":"Name the network, not the owner: Wi-Fi密码, not 我的密码."},
"hotel|两百？太贵了，一百吧！":{"why":"押金 yājīn is a refundable deposit, a standard practice at mainland hotels, and 退给您 tuì gěi nín means \"we'll give it back to you\". 太贵了 tài guì le treats it like a price, and haggling over it sounds odd because you lose nothing. These days you usually just scan to pay, 我扫码 wǒ sǎo mǎ, and the hold is released when you check out.","tip":"押金 comes back when you 退房, so there's nothing to bargain."},
"hotel|没问题，大床房。":{"why":"大床房 dàchuáng fáng is a room with one big bed, and 双床房 shuāngchuáng fáng has two beds, 双 shuāng meaning a pair. She said the big-bed room is 满了 mǎn le, full, so asking for it again cancels your yes. 也行 yě xíng, \"that works too\", is a handy way to accept a swap.","tip":"双 shuāng means a pair, so 双床房 means two beds."},
"hotel|我是大床房。":{"why":"是 shì links two things that are the same, so 我是大床房 wǒ shì dàchuáng fáng literally claims you are a room. Chinese can sometimes use 是 loosely in this way (like 我是咖啡 wǒ shì kāfēi when ordering), but here it sounds like you're insisting on the room she just said is full. Since the task is to accept, 可以 kěyǐ does the job.","tip":"When someone asks 可以吗, the shortest yes is 可以."},
"hotel|三天。":{"why":"Hotels count stays by 晚 wǎn, nights, and the question 住几晚 zhù jǐ wǎn already gave you the word. 三天 sān tiān is understandable, but people may wonder whether you mean three nights or three days with two nights. Echoing her measure word removes any doubt.","tip":"Hotels sell nights, so count with 晚 wǎn."},
"hotel|三点。":{"why":"点 diǎn means o'clock, so 三点 sān diǎn sounds like you're telling her a time. 几 jǐ means \"how many\" and the word after it tells you what's being counted, here 晚 wǎn, nights. Answer with the same word: 三晚 sān wǎn.","tip":"Answer 几晚 with a number plus 晚, just swap 几 for your number."},
"hotel|五星？我要五星的房间。":{"why":"五星好评 wǔ xīng hǎopíng is a five-star good review, and 好评 hǎopíng is literally \"good comment\". Hotels and shops in China often ask for reviews on apps like Meituan or Ctrip, so this is a common request. 能给我们 néng gěi wǒmen means she wants you to give something to them, not the other way round.","tip":"好评 is a good review: when you hear 评 píng, think rating."},
"friend|我是英国。":{"why":"Chinese nationalities are built by adding 人 rén, person, to the country: 英国 Yīngguó Britain becomes 英国人 Yīngguó rén, British. Without 人, 是 shì links you directly to the country. Locals will understand, but it sounds a little like \"I am the UK\".","tip":"Country + 人 = a person from there."},
"friend|我在英国。":{"why":"在 zài tells where someone is right now, so 我在英国 wǒ zài Yīngguó says you're currently in Britain, which is odd while you're chatting in China. 哪国人 nǎ guó rén asks which country's person you are, so the answer follows the same frame: 我是英国人 wǒ shì Yīngguó rén.","tip":"Fill in the blank of the question: 你是哪国人 becomes 我是英国人."},
"friend|谢谢！":{"why":"谢谢 xièxie is perfectly normal, and younger urban Chinese say it all the time. 哪里哪里 nǎlǐ nǎlǐ literally means \"where? where?\", as in \"where is this good Chinese you speak of?\", a modest brush-off with a long tradition. From a foreigner it gets smiles because it shows you know the culture, not just the words.","tip":"哪里哪里 is \"where, where?\": you're politely searching for the praise you can't see."},
"friend|对，我的中文很好。":{"why":"Chinese etiquette prizes modesty, so openly agreeing with a compliment can come across as arrogant, even as a joke. The expected move is to deflect: 哪里哪里 nǎlǐ nǎlǐ, or 还差得远呢 hái chà de yuǎn ne, \"I'm still far off\", where 差 chà means short of the mark. The 呢 ne at the end makes it sound relaxed and friendly.","tip":"Praise in, modesty out: answer a compliment by pointing at how far you still have to go."},
"friend|好，加油！":{"why":"加 jiā means add, and 加微信 jiā Wēixìn is the standard way to say \"add on WeChat\". 加油 jiāyóu literally means \"add oil\", like fuelling a car, and is what people shout to cheer someone on. So 好，加油 hǎo, jiāyóu sounds like you're wishing them luck instead of agreeing to swap contacts.","tip":"加 + what you're adding: 加微信 adds a contact, 加油 adds fuel."},
"friend|可以，我的电话是……":{"why":"In China, WeChat is the main way people stay in touch, and it's added by scanning a QR code, not typing a phone number. The usual question is who scans whom: 我扫你 wǒ sǎo nǐ means you scan their code, 你扫我 nǐ sǎo wǒ means they scan yours. Offering a phone number still works, it just feels like an extra step.","tip":"扫 sǎo (scan) goes from the scanner to the code: 我扫你 means I scan yours."},
"friend|三月。":{"why":"Chinese month names are just number + 月 yuè, so 三月 sānyuè is the third month, March. To count months as a length of time, you need the measure word 个 ge: 三个月 sān ge yuè, three months. It's a tiny word with a big effect, and he'd hear your answer as \"in March\".","tip":"With 个 it's a stretch of time, without 个 it's a name on the calendar."},
"friend|我来三个月。":{"why":"In 来了三个月了 lái le sān ge yuè le, the first 了 le shows the arrival happened, and the final 了 le says the situation continues up to now, like the English \"have been\". 我来三个月 wǒ lái sān ge yuè sounds like a plan, \"I'll come for three months\". The double 了 is a common pattern for durations: 学了两年了 xué le liǎng nián le, \"I've been studying for two years\".","tip":"Verb + 了 + time + 了 means \"have been doing it this long, and still going\"."},
"friend|我五十二岁。":{"why":"Chinese numbers read like a little formula: 二十五 èrshíwǔ is two tens plus five, so the tens digit always comes first, then 十 shí, then the ones. 五十二 wǔshí'èr is five tens plus two, 52. It's the same order as English digits, so read the numerals left to right and say them in that order.","tip":"Say the digits as you see them, slotting 十 shí in between: 2-十-5."},
"friend|我有二十五岁。":{"why":"Age in Chinese works like a description, not a possession, so you say 我二十五岁 wǒ èrshíwǔ suì, with no verb at all. 有 yǒu sounds like a carryover from \"I have 25 years\" in languages like French or Spanish. You might hear 有 used for estimates, as in 他有五十岁了吧 tā yǒu wǔshí suì le ba, \"he must be about fifty\", but not when you state your own age.","tip":"Age is just number + 岁 suì: no verb in between."},
"friend|可以，多少钱？":{"why":"互相学习 hùxiāng xuéxí, learning from each other, is a language exchange where each person teaches their own language for free. Asking 多少钱 duōshao qián turns it into a paid lesson and can make the other person feel awkward or a bit hurt. Matching their friendly tone with 好啊 hǎo a and repeating their phrase sounds warm.","tip":"互相 hùxiāng means mutual, both ways, so there's nothing to pay."},
"friend|我也学英语。":{"why":"She told you she's learning English, 我在学英语 wǒ zài xué Yīngyǔ, and 也 yě means \"also\", so 我也学英语 wǒ yě xué Yīngyǔ says you're learning English too, which makes the exchange pointless. 英语 Yīngyǔ is English and 中文 Zhōngwén is Chinese. She was really asking for a yes, so 好啊 hǎo a is all you needed.","tip":"也 yě copies what the other person just said, so use it only if you really do the same thing."},
"bill|你好！":{"why":"你好 nǐ hǎo works in a restaurant, especially in nicer places or with younger staff. 服务员 fúwùyuán, \"service person\", is the everyday call in most restaurants and is not considered rude. In a busy room, a clear 服务员 with a raised hand is the quickest way to get someone's attention.","tip":"服务员 fúwùyuán is a job title, so calling it out is as normal as saying \"excuse me\"."},
"bill|喂，小姑娘！":{"why":"喂 wèi is what you say answering the phone; shouted at a person it sounds like \"hey, you!\". 小姑娘 xiǎo gūniang, \"little girl\", is what older people might call a young woman, but from a customer it can sound patronising. 服务员 fúwùyuán or 你好 nǐ hǎo keep it neutral and respectful.","tip":"Save 喂 wèi for the phone and use 服务员 for people."},
"bill|我要买单子。":{"why":"买单 mǎidān is a set phrase meaning \"pay the bill\", even though its parts mean \"buy\" and \"list\". Splitting it into 买 + 单子 dānzi makes it literal: \"I want to buy a piece of paper\". Saying the word on its own, 买单, is short and completely natural. You might also hear 结账 jiézhàng, which means the same thing.","tip":"买单 is one word, like \"check please\", so don't take it apart."},
"bill|多少钱？":{"why":"多少钱 duōshao qián asks the price, and works for ordering or shopping, so the waiter might just tell you the total. 买单 mǎidān signals you're finished and ready to pay, and they'll bring the bill or a code to scan. 多少钱 still gets you there, it just takes a step longer.","tip":"When you're done eating, 买单 is your one-word exit."},
"bill|一起。":{"why":"一起付 yìqǐ fù means \"pay together\", one payment covering the whole table. 分开付 fēnkāi fù means paying separately. Since you wanted to split it four ways, 一起 yìqǐ would leave one person footing the full 240.","tip":"分开 fēnkāi means split apart, 一起 yìqǐ means all in one."},
"bill|我们四个人分。":{"why":"分 fēn means to divide, so 我们四个人分 wǒmen sì ge rén fēn is clear. The everyday phrase, though, is AA, as in 我们AA吧 wǒmen AA ba, meaning \"let's split it evenly\". It's said with English letters and is used everywhere from restaurants to taxis.","tip":"Two letters, one bill split down the middle: AA."},
"bill|好的。":{"why":"要不要 yào bu yào is an A-not-A question, \"want or not want?\", so 好的 hǎo de, \"okay\", is a yes. The waiter will bring dessert. To turn it down politely, 不用了 bú yòng le means \"no need\", and adding 我们吃饱了 wǒmen chī bǎo le, \"we're full\", gives a friendly reason.","tip":"To say no to an offer, start with 不用了 and add a reason."},
"bill|不要。":{"why":"不要 bú yào is a flat \"don't want\", which is clear but can sound curt, especially to a friendly waiter. 不用了 bú yòng le, \"no need\", turns down an offer while thanking them. A short reason like 吃饱了 chī bǎo le softens it even more.","tip":"不用了 sounds like \"no, thank you\"; 不要 sounds like \"no\"."},
"bill|好，你扫我吧。":{"why":"你扫我 nǐ sǎo wǒ means \"you scan my code\", the staff scanning your payment QR code. She said 扫桌上的二维码 sǎo zhuō shang de èrwéimǎ, scan the QR code on the table, which is how many Chinese restaurants handle orders and payments. 我们扫 wǒmen sǎo puts you in charge of the scanning.","tip":"The one before 扫 does the scanning: 我扫 means I scan."},
"bill|什么码？":{"why":"二维码 èrwéimǎ means QR code, literally \"two-dimension code\". She added 桌上 zhuō shang, on the table, so the code is printed or stuck on your table. Asking 什么码 shénme mǎ is a reasonable check, and catching 桌上 next time saves a step.","tip":"When you hear 桌上 zhuō shang, look down at the table."},
"bill|很好喝。":{"why":"Chinese has separate words for tasty depending on how you consume it: 好吃 hǎochī for food, 好喝 hǎohē for drinks. 很好喝 hěn hǎohē, \"very nice to drink\", sounds like you only enjoyed the soup or tea. The same pattern gives you 好看 hǎokàn for good-looking and 好听 hǎotīng for nice to hear.","tip":"好 + the verb you used: eat 吃, drink 喝."},
"bill|还可以。":{"why":"还可以 hái kěyǐ, \"it's still okay\", is mild and can sound unenthusiastic, like \"it was fine, I guess\". If the food was good, the cook would love 很好吃 hěn hǎochī. Chinese hosts often take lukewarm feedback to heart, so a warm compliment goes a long way.","tip":"还 hái here means \"just about\", so 还可以 is barely a pass."},
"tea|我要大的奶茶，有球的。":{"why":"Your description worked, but 珍珠 zhēnzhū, pearls, is the standard name for the tapioca balls, so a pearl milk tea is 珍珠奶茶 zhēnzhū nǎichá. Drinks are sized by cup, 杯 bēi, so large is 大杯 dà bēi, and medium is 中杯 zhōng bēi. Putting size first, 大杯珍珠奶茶, matches what's on the menu.","tip":"Size by cup first, 大杯 dà bēi, then the drink name."},
"tea|我喝奶茶了。":{"why":"Putting 了 le at the end of 我喝奶茶了 wǒ hē nǎichá le says the drinking has already happened, like \"I drank milk tea\". To order, Chinese uses 要 yào, want, with no 了: 要一个大杯的 yào yí ge dà bēi de. 要 is the standard ordering verb in shops and cafes.","tip":"Ordering is wanting, so start with 要 yào."},
"tea|小糖。":{"why":"小 xiǎo and 少 shǎo are both third tone and differ only in the x versus sh sound, so they're easy to mix. 小 describes size, small, while 少 describes amount, less or few. Tea shops print 少糖 shǎo táng, 半糖 bàn táng (half sugar) and 无糖 wú táng (no sugar) on the menu, so 小糖 sounds like \"tiny sugar\".","tip":"少 shǎo is about how much, 小 xiǎo is about how big: sugar is an amount."},
"tea|正常。":{"why":"正常 zhèngcháng means normal, and at a tea shop 正常糖 zhèngcháng táng is the full standard sweetness, which many people find very sweet. She offered you a choice between two options with 还是 háishi, \"or\", so the word you echo becomes your order. Since you wanted less, the second option, 少糖 shǎo táng, was the one to repeat.","tip":"With a 还是 question, repeat the option you want, word for word."},
"tea|是。":{"why":"还是 háishi means \"or\" in a question offering a choice, so 冰的还是热的 bīng de háishi rè de is \"iced or hot?\". It's not a yes-or-no question, so 是 shì doesn't answer anything. Pick one of the options and add any extras: 冰的，少冰 bīng de, shǎo bīng.","tip":"还是 háishi asks you to choose, so answer with a choice, not a yes."},
"tea|冰，小冰。":{"why":"Same as with sugar, 小 xiǎo describes size and 少 shǎo describes amount, so less ice is 少冰 shǎo bīng. 小冰 xiǎo bīng sounds like \"small ice cubes\". You'll also hear 去冰 qù bīng, no ice, and 常温 chángwēn, room temperature.","tip":"Sugar and ice are amounts, so both take 少 shǎo."},
"tea|好，我要珍珠。":{"why":"完 wán means finished, so 卖完了 mài wán le is \"sold until finished\", sold out. The verb + 完 pattern shows up everywhere: 吃完了 chī wán le, finished eating; 用完了 yòng wán le, used up. She was offering 椰果 yēguǒ, coconut jelly, instead, and 我要珍珠 wǒ yào zhēnzhū asks for what she just said they don't have.","tip":"Verb + 完 wán means there's none left to verb."},
"tea|没关系。":{"why":"没关系 méi guānxi is the right answer to her 不好意思 bù hǎoyìsi, but her actual question was 换椰果可以吗 huàn yēguǒ kěyǐ ma, \"is coconut jelly okay?\". A 可以吗 question is answered with 可以 kěyǐ or 不可以 bù kěyǐ. 可以 on its own accepts her offer.","tip":"Answer 可以吗 with 可以."},
"tea|四分钟？好的。":{"why":"四 sì is 4 and 四十 sìshí is 40; the only difference is the second syllable 十 shí. 四 sì and 十 shí are also easily confused with each other because of the s and sh sounds. 四十分钟 sìshí fēnzhōng is 40 minutes, a long wait, but good to catch before you agree.","tip":"Count the syllables: one syllable is 4, two syllables is 40."},
"tea|等等。":{"why":"等等 děngdeng repeats the verb to soften it into \"hang on a moment\", so it sounds like you're asking her to wait. 我等 wǒ děng, \"I'll wait\", puts you as the one waiting. 没事 méi shì, \"no problem\", in front makes it sound relaxed.","tip":"等等 asks them to wait; 我等 says you will."},
"tea|会，我会。":{"why":"会员 huìyuán means member, where 会 here means an association or club, a different meaning from 会 huì, \"can\" or \"know how to\". Chinese words often share a first character, so listen for the second syllable. 是会员吗 shì huìyuán ma is a 是 question, so the answer is 是 shì or 不是 bú shì.","tip":"会员 huìyuán is a club member, 会 huì alone is a skill."},
"tea|没有。":{"why":"Chinese answers usually echo the verb of the question: 是…吗 shì…ma gets 是 or 不是, and 有…吗 yǒu…ma gets 有 or 没有. 没有 méiyǒu sounds like \"I don't have one\", which she probably took to mean no card, so she understood. 不是 bú shì matches her question exactly.","tip":"Echo the verb: a 是 question gets 是 or 不是."},
"haircut|洗。":{"why":"洗 xǐ is wash and 剪 jiǎn is cut. She offered 剪头发 jiǎn tóufa, a cut, or 洗头 xǐ tóu, a wash, with 还是 háishi meaning or. Picking 洗 means you've just asked for a wash.","tip":"剪 jiǎn has 刀 dāo, knife, at the bottom: that's the one that cuts."},
"haircut|我要切头发。":{"why":"Chinese uses different verbs for cutting depending on the tool. 切 qiē is cutting with a knife, like slicing meat or vegetables, while 剪 jiǎn is cutting with scissors, used for hair, nails and paper. 切头发 qiē tóufa sounds a bit like you want your hair chopped with a kitchen knife.","tip":"Scissors 剪 jiǎn, knife 切 qiē: hairdressers use scissors."},
"haircut|剪光。":{"why":"光 guāng means bare or nothing left, so 剪光 jiǎn guāng is cutting it all off, like a buzz cut or shaved head. Verb + 光 shows up in 吃光 chī guāng, ate it all. For a light trim, say 短一点 duǎn yìdiǎn, a bit shorter.","tip":"光 guāng is light: once it's 剪光, light shines on your scalp."},
"haircut|小一点。":{"why":"小 xiǎo describes size, so 小一点 xiǎo yìdiǎn sounds like a smaller head of hair, more volume than length. 短 duǎn means short in length, and 短一点 duǎn yìdiǎn, \"a bit shorter\", is exactly what barbers expect. Its opposite is 长 cháng, long.","tip":"Length is 短 duǎn and 长 cháng, size is 小 xiǎo and 大 dà."},
"haircut|好的好的。":{"why":"好的好的 hǎo de hǎo de is an enthusiastic yes, so the stylist will start setting up a membership card. Chinese hair salons are known for pushing 充值 chōngzhí, prepaid top-ups, often with deals like 充五百送一百 chōng wǔbǎi sòng yìbǎi, top up 500 and get 100 free. 不用了，谢谢 bú yòng le, xièxie turns it down kindly.","tip":"Hear 充 chōng in a salon? Get ready to say 不用了."},
"haircut|不要卡。":{"why":"不要卡 bú yào kǎ is clear but can sound flat or even a bit cold, like \"don't want the card\". 不用了 bú yòng le, \"no need\", frames it as you're fine without it, and adding 谢谢 xièxie thanks them for the offer. That softer version usually ends the sales pitch faster.","tip":"不用了 sounds like \"no, thank you\"; 不要 sounds like \"no\"."},
"haircut|我不要洗头，我要剪头发。":{"why":"先…再… xiān…zài… is a sequence pattern: first this, then that. 洗完再剪 xǐ wán zài jiǎn means \"after washing, then cut\". In many Chinese salons, a wash comes first as part of the cut, sometimes with a scalp massage. Saying you don't want a wash sounds like you misunderstood the routine.","tip":"先 xiān first, 再 zài next: two steps in order."},
"haircut|现在洗？":{"why":"先 xiān means first, so the wash comes now, before the cut. 现在洗 xiànzài xǐ is a reasonable check, but 好，我先去洗头 hǎo, wǒ xiān qù xǐ tóu shows you got it. Picking up her 先 makes you sound tuned in.","tip":"先 xiān is your cue: whatever follows it happens now."},
"haircut|有点汤。":{"why":"汤 tāng and 烫 tàng differ only in tone: first-tone tāng stays high and flat, fourth-tone tàng drops sharply. 汤 is soup and 烫 is scalding hot, so 有点汤 yǒudiǎn tāng sounds like \"there's a bit of soup\". The falling tone even sounds like someone reacting to something hot.","tip":"Say 烫 tàng with a sharp drop, like jerking your hand from hot water."},
"haircut|很热。":{"why":"热 rè is general heat, like hot weather or a hot dish, while 烫 tàng means hot enough to burn or sting when it touches you. Water on your scalp, hot tea or a pan handle are all 烫. 有点烫 yǒudiǎn tàng is the usual way to say \"a bit too hot\" during a wash.","tip":"热 is in the air, 烫 is on your skin."},
"haircut|好，再短一点。":{"why":"He said 剪得有点短了 jiǎn de yǒudiǎn duǎn le, \"I cut it a bit too short\", and 没关系吧 méi guānxi ba asks for reassurance. 再 zài means again or more, so 再短一点 zài duǎn yìdiǎn asks for even more off. 会长出来的 huì zhǎng chūlái de, \"it'll grow back\", uses 长 zhǎng, to grow, not cháng, long.","tip":"再 zài means more of the same, so only use it if you want more."},
"haircut|太短了！":{"why":"太短了 tài duǎn le is honest, but after his apology it sounds like a complaint. In Chinese, when someone has already admitted a mistake, replying 没关系 méi guānxi helps them save face. 会长出来的 huì zhǎng chūlái de, \"it'll grow back\", adds a nice reassuring touch.","tip":"Once someone says sorry, 没关系 lets them off the hook."},
"sim|我要买手机。":{"why":"手机 shǒujī, \"hand machine\", is a mobile phone. The SIM card is 电话卡 diànhuà kǎ, phone card, or 手机卡 shǒujī kǎ. Phone shops sell both, so 我要买手机 wǒ yào mǎi shǒujī sounds like you're there to buy a new phone.","tip":"机 jī is the machine, 卡 kǎ is the card: you want the 卡."},
"sim|我要一个电话卡。":{"why":"Cards are flat, so they take 张 zhāng, like tickets and paper. 办 bàn means to handle or process, and is used for getting official things like cards, visas and memberships, since a SIM needs registration. 我要一个电话卡 wǒ yào yí ge diànhuà kǎ is clear, but 办一张 bàn yì zhāng sounds like you know the process.","tip":"Paperwork things get 办 bàn, flat things get 张 zhāng."},
"sim|我不带。":{"why":"不带 bú dài sounds like you refuse to bring it, \"I won't bring it\", which is a strange thing to say to staff. Her question 带护照了吗 dài hùzhào le ma uses 了 le, asking if you did bring it. Since you have it, echo the verb with 了: 带了 dài le, then 给您 gěi nín as you hand it over. If you had left it behind, 没带 méi dài would be the answer.","tip":"A 了吗 question gets 带了 for yes and 没带 for no."},
"sim|有。":{"why":"有 yǒu, \"have\", is understood, but she asked with 带 dài, bring, so echoing her verb sounds more natural. Chinese yes-or-no questions are usually answered by repeating the verb, so 带了 dài le, \"brought it\", is the most native response. It's a small change but it makes you sound fluent.","tip":"Chinese has no single word for yes: repeat the question's verb."},
"sim|一百二十九的。":{"why":"一百二十九 yìbǎi èrshíjiǔ is 129, the pricier plan. In Chinese, a 的 de after a price, like 五十九的 wǔshíjiǔ de, means \"the 59 one\". Big numbers are read like they're written: 一百 yìbǎi (100), 二十 èrshí (20), 九 jiǔ (9).","tip":"Read the price from the left: 一百 is the hundreds, so it's the expensive one."},
"sim|便宜的。":{"why":"便宜的 piányi de, \"the cheap one\", is clear, but naming the price, 五十九的 wǔshíjiǔ de, rules out any mix-up. Adding 就行 jiù xíng, \"will do\", sounds natural and relaxed. Staff often hear numbers more reliably than adjectives.","tip":"Name the price, not the adjective."},
"sim|去年。":{"why":"去年 qùnián, \"gone year\", is last year, a point in time, while 多久 duō jiǔ asks how long. A length answer uses number + time word: 一年 yì nián, one year. 待 dāi means to stay somewhere for a while, so 待一年 dāi yì nián is the full reply.","tip":"多久 asks for a length: answer with a number, not a date."},
"sim|一个年。":{"why":"年 nián, year, is one of a handful of time words that don't need a measure word, along with 天 tiān, day. So it's 一年 yì nián and 两年 liǎng nián, while months do need 个: 一个月 yí ge yuè. 一个年 yí ge nián sounds like a counting slip.","tip":"Days and years count bare, months need a 个."},
"sim|好，要带八的。":{"why":"八 bā sounds like 发 fā, as in 发财 fācái (to get rich), so numbers full of eights are treated as lucky and sold at a premium. In 带八的 dài bā de, 带 means 'containing', and the clerk's 多五十块 duō wǔshí kuài puts 多 before an amount to mean 'extra'. 不加钱的 bù jiā qián de uses 的 to mean 'the kind that doesn't add money', a handy pattern for picking from any range. Funnily enough, numbers with 四 sì are often the cheap ones, since it sounds like 死 sǐ, death.","tip":"Eights cost extra because 八 bā rhymes with 发 fā, get rich: luck isn't free."},
"sim|有，我有卡。":{"why":"Chinese builds new words by stacking old ones: 卡 kǎ (card) plus 针 zhēn (needle) gives 卡针 kǎzhēn, the little pin that pops out the SIM tray. If you only catch the first syllable you will often grab the wrong word, because 卡 also starts 卡片 kǎpiàn, 银行卡 yínhángkǎ and more. Her second sentence was the clue: 我们的找不到了 wǒmen de zhǎo bu dào le, 'we can't find ours', which only makes sense for a tool, not your card.","tip":"针 zhēn is a needle: picture it poking the card tray."},
"sim|不是。":{"why":"Mandarin has no all-purpose yes or no; you answer by echoing the question's verb. 有…吗 gets 有 yǒu or 没有 méiyǒu, while 是…吗 gets 是 shì or 不是 bú shì. 有 is special: it is only ever negated with 没 méi, so 不有 does not exist. 不是 instead sounds like 'that's not it', as if you were correcting what she said.","tip":"有 only ever pairs with 没: 没有, never 不有."},
"lost|我丢了。":{"why":"丢 diū means 'to lose' or 'to go missing', and on its own 我丢了 wǒ diū le leaves the listener waiting for what. Mandarin likes to put the missing thing first as the topic: 我的手机丢了 wǒ de shǒujī diū le, literally 'my phone, gone'. 了 le marks the change: it was there, now it isn't. And if you yourself are lost, that's a different word entirely, 迷路 mílù.","tip":"Put the lost thing first: 手机丢了, phone gone."},
"lost|我没有手机。":{"why":"没有 méiyǒu plus a noun is about possession, so 我没有手机 wǒ méiyǒu shǒujī sounds like a fact about your life, maybe a lead-in to borrowing hers. 丢了 diū le adds the story: you had it, and now it's gone. Another very natural option is 我手机找不到了 wǒ shǒujī zhǎo bu dào le, 'I can't find my phone'.","tip":"没有 is never had; 丢了 is had, then gone."},
"lost|地铁在二号线。":{"why":"X 在 Y says where X is located, so 地铁在二号线 dìtiě zài èr hào xiàn tells her where the metro lives, not where you were. To say where something happened, start with 在 plus the place: 在二号线的地铁上 zài èr hào xiàn de dìtiě shang. Vehicles take 上 shang, just like English says 'on' the train: 车上 chē shang, 飞机上 fēijī shang.","tip":"Answer 在哪儿 with 在 plus a place: 在…上."},
"lost|二号线。":{"why":"Dropping words in a quick answer is perfectly normal in Mandarin, so 二号线 èr hào xiàn gets through. The catch is that staff want to know if it was on a train, on a platform or in a station, since those are searched differently. 在二号线的地铁上 zài èr hào xiàn de dìtiě shang pins it to the train itself, and 上 shang is said lightly here.","tip":"Trains, buses and planes all take 上: 车上, 地铁上, 飞机上."},
"lost|红色的苹果。":{"why":"苹果 Píngguǒ is both the fruit and the brand, and when phones are already the topic locals do often say just 苹果. The real trouble is the colour: you said 红色的 hóngsè de, so she'll look for a red phone, when the phone is black and only the case is red. Saying 苹果手机 Píngguǒ shǒujī removes any doubt, and the case is 手机壳 shǒujī ké.","tip":"Brand plus 手机: 苹果手机, 华为手机 Huáwéi shǒujī."},
"lost|黑手机，红壳。":{"why":"One-syllable colours like 黑 hēi and 红 hóng can sit right before a noun, so 黑手机 hēi shǒujī isn't wrong. But when describing something for a search, people reach for 黑色的 hēisè de, 'black-coloured', which sounds fuller and clearer. 壳 on its own is a bit bare; 手机壳 shǒujī ké tells her exactly which shell you mean. In everyday speech 壳 is ké, though you may see the bookish reading qiào in dictionaries.","tip":"Describing for a search? Add 色的: 黑色的, 红色的."},
"lost|不用谢。":{"why":"不用谢 bú yòng xiè is the reply to someone thanking you, so it sounds like you're answering a thank-you nobody gave. Worse, it starts with 不用 bú yòng, 'no need', which is exactly how people decline an offer. Her 要不要 yào bu yào question wants 好 or 要 first, then your thanks: 太谢谢你了 tài xièxie nǐ le, where 太…了 turns the thanks up.","tip":"不用 starts a no; 好啊 starts a yes."},
"lost|可以。":{"why":"可以 kěyǐ is the language of permission, 'you may', so it can sound like you're allowing her to help rather than gratefully accepting. 好啊 hǎo a, with that soft 啊, sounds warm and keen. Adding 太谢谢你了 tài xièxie nǐ le shows you know she's going out of her way.","tip":"可以 grants permission; 好啊 accepts a kindness."},
"lost|三个半小时。":{"why":"半 bàn attaches differently for clock time and for duration. 三点半 sān diǎn bàn is 3:30, a point on the clock, while 三个半小时 sān ge bàn xiǎoshí is a stretch of three and a half hours. The question word gives it away: 几点 jǐ diǎn asks what time, while 几个小时 jǐ ge xiǎoshí asks how long.","tip":"点 is a point on the clock; 小时 is a stretch of time."},
"lost|下午。":{"why":"Chinese time goes from big to small: part of day, then hour, then minutes, as in 下午三点半 xiàwǔ sān diǎn bàn. 下午 xiàwǔ alone covers a four- or five-hour window, too wide for a search. Since 几点 jǐ diǎn literally asks 'how many o'clock', the answer needs a 点.","tip":"Big to small: 下午, then 三点, then 半."},
"lost|是！太好了！":{"why":"是你的吗 shì nǐ de ma is a plain yes-or-no question, and the detail that decides it came first: 白色 báisè, white. In a lost-property office, claiming the wrong phone wastes everyone's time and someone else's phone goes home with you. 我的是黑色的 wǒ de shì hēisè de, 'mine is a black one', uses 的 twice to stand in for 'phone'.","tip":"Before you say 是, check the colour word."},
"lost|我不是。":{"why":"To answer a yes-or-no question you echo the verb and usually drop the subject: 是你的吗 gets 是 or 不是 bú shì. Adding 我 makes you the topic, so 我不是 wǒ bú shì sounds like 'I'm not (something)'. The subject of her question was the phone, so 不是我的 bú shì wǒ de, 'not mine', keeps the focus where it belongs. 不 becomes bú here because 是 shì is fourth tone.","tip":"Echo the verb, drop the 我: 是你的吗 gets 不是."},
"toilet|厕所在哪儿？":{"why":"厕所 cèsuǒ is the plain, everyday word and it's on plenty of signs, so nobody is offended. 洗手间 xǐshǒujiān, 'hand-washing room', is the gentler word, much like 'restroom' in English, and 卫生间 wèishēngjiān is also common in homes and hotels. Starting with 请问 qǐngwèn, 'may I ask', is the standard polite way to stop a stranger.","tip":"厕所 for the street, 洗手间 for the mall."},
"toilet|哪儿是洗澡间？":{"why":"洗 xǐ means wash, and what follows says what's being washed: 洗手 xǐshǒu is hands, 洗澡 xǐzǎo is the whole body. Add 间 jiān, room, and one character swaps a toilet for a shower room. Word order matters too: Mandarin likes the thing first and 在哪儿 at the end, as in 洗手间在哪儿 xǐshǒujiān zài nǎr.","tip":"手 shǒu is hand: the hand-washing room is the toilet."},
"toilet|电梯里面？":{"why":"Position words come after the noun in Mandarin, so 'next to the lift' is 电梯旁边 diàntī pángbiān, literally 'lift's side'. 里面 lǐmiàn means inside, which would put the toilet in the lift itself. Other useful ones are 对面 duìmiàn (opposite) and 后面 hòumiàn (behind). Ending with 对吧 duì ba, 'right?', is a natural way to check.","tip":"旁 páng is the side; 里 lǐ is in."},
"toilet|两楼？":{"why":"两 liǎng counts how many, as in 两个 liǎng ge or 两斤 liǎng jīn, while 二 èr names which one in a series. Floors, bus lines and metro lines are names, so they take 二: 二楼 èr lóu, 二号线 èr hào xiàn. 两楼 sounds like 'two floors' or 'two buildings', so the guard has to stop and think.","tip":"How many uses 两; which one uses 二."},
"toilet|什么纸？":{"why":"纸 zhǐ on its own is just paper, but asked outside a toilet, 你带纸了吗 means toilet paper, and many public toilets in China don't stock any. Asking what they mean is fine, and they'll tell you, but the real answer is about you: 没带 méi dài, I didn't bring any, then 哪儿能买纸巾 nǎr néng mǎi zhǐjīn, where can I buy tissues.","tip":"Near a toilet, 纸 means toilet paper: answer 带了 or 没带."},
"toilet|带了！":{"why":"带 dài means to bring or carry along, so 你带纸了吗 nǐ dài zhǐ le ma gets 带了 dài le for yes and 没带 méi dài for no. 纸 zhǐ here means toilet paper, and many public toilets in China have none, so locals carry a pack of 纸巾 zhǐjīn (tissues). If a word slips past you, 什么纸 shénme zhǐ, 'what paper?', is far safer than a cheerful yes.","tip":"带了吗 gets 带了 or 没带, and always carry a tissue pack."},
"toilet|楼上在哪儿？":{"why":"楼上 lóushàng is upstairs, a place, and 楼下 lóuxià is downstairs. The stairs themselves are 楼梯 lóutī, which shares 梯 tī, ladder, with 电梯 diàntī, the 'electric ladder' or lift. His 走楼梯 zǒu lóutī means 'take the stairs', with 走 meaning to go by a route.","tip":"梯 tī is a ladder: electric ladder 电梯, building ladder 楼梯."},
"toilet|电梯在哪儿？":{"why":"His sentence opened with 电梯 diàntī as the topic, 电梯人太多了 diàntī rén tài duō le, 'the lift, too many people', so it's the word that sticks. The actual advice came at the end: 走楼梯快一点 zǒu lóutī kuài yìdiǎn, where 快一点 means 'a bit quicker'. 电梯 and 楼梯 lóutī differ only in their first syllable, so listen for 楼 lóu versus 电 diàn.","tip":"Advice usually lands at the end of the sentence: catch the last noun."},
"toilet|四楼，对吧？":{"why":"三 sān is high and level with an open -an, while 四 sì drops sharply and ends in a buzzy -i, almost like a hissing z. If you hear the voice fall, it's four; if it stays up, it's three. Repeating numbers back, as you did with 对吧 duì ba, is a great habit, so the guard can catch the slip.","tip":"三 sān stays up high; 四 sì drops like a stone."},
"toilet|好的，我去二楼。":{"why":"哦对了 ò duì le, 'oh right', is a signal that new information is coming, so it's a cue to listen harder. 在 zài before a verb means something is in progress, so 在维修 zài wéixiū is 'under repair'. The 吧 ba in 你去三楼吧 nǐ qù sān lóu ba makes it a suggestion, and starting your reply with 那 nà, 'in that case', shows you've taken in the change.","tip":"对了 means 'oh, by the way': new info is next."},
"toilet|习惯。":{"why":"Mandarin answers yes-or-no questions by repeating the verb, so 习惯 xíguàn on its own means 'yes, I'm used to them'. For no, just put 不 in front: 不习惯 bù xíguàn. Then 有坐的吗 yǒu zuò de ma asks for a sit-down one, with 的 standing in for 'toilet'. Most malls have at least one, often the accessible toilet.","tip":"Repeat the verb for yes; put 不 in front for no."},
"toilet|我不会蹲。":{"why":"会 huì is about a learned skill, so 我不会蹲 wǒ bú huì dūn sounds like 'I never learned how to squat', which is why it gets a laugh. 不习惯 bù xíguàn, 'not used to it', is about habit and fits better. Even simpler is to skip the explanation and ask for what you need: 有坐的吗 yǒu zuò de ma.","tip":"不会 is can't-do; 不习惯 is not-used-to."},
"fruit|橘子多少钱？":{"why":"At Chinese markets prices are quoted per 斤 jīn, half a kilo, by default. The word order is the reverse of English: 多少钱一斤 duōshao qián yì jīn, 'how much money, one jin'. Notice 一 is said yì here, because the next syllable 斤 is first tone.","tip":"Money first, unit last: 多少钱一斤."},
"fruit|橘子多少钱一公斤？":{"why":"公斤 gōngjīn is the official kilo and one 公斤 is exactly two 斤 jīn. Stall price signs nearly always use 斤, so asking per 公斤 makes the seller do maths and sounds a bit textbook. When you see 6块一斤 liù kuài yì jīn, double it to get the kilo price.","tip":"One 公斤 is two 斤: double the stall price for a kilo."},
"fruit|要二斤。":{"why":"Before a measure word, the number two is usually 两 liǎng: 两个 liǎng ge, 两杯 liǎng bēi, 两斤 liǎng jīn. 二 èr is for counting, digits and ordering, like 二楼 èr lóu. You will hear 二斤 from some northern speakers, since 斤 is an old traditional unit, but 两斤 is the standard you can't go wrong with.","tip":"Number before a measure word? Two becomes 两."},
"fruit|要十斤。":{"why":"十 shí rises with a curled sh, while 两 liǎng dips low and ends in -ng, so they don't sound alike once you hear them side by side. Because a 斤 jīn is half a kilo, 十斤 shí jīn is five kilos, a whole heavy bag of mandarins. She would probably double-check, but saying it right saves the confusion.","tip":"十 shí rises like a question; 两 liǎng dips low."},
"fruit|太贵了，不要了！":{"why":"Fruit-stall bargaining is gentle and mostly about rounding: once it's weighed at 十三块二 shísān kuài èr, the natural ask is to drop the small change. 不要了 bú yào le, 'don't want it any more', after she's bagged and weighed it means she has to unpack, so it lands as a small scene. 便宜一点行吗 piányi yìdiǎn xíng ma, or 抹个零吧 mǒ ge líng ba, 'knock off the odd bit', keeps it friendly.","tip":"For loose change, say 抹个零吧: drop the odd jiao."},
"fruit|我不要钱。":{"why":"不甜不要钱 bù tián bú yào qián is a classic vendor line built on 不A不B, 'if not A, then not B', a conditional with no 'if' word at all. 先尝一个 xiān cháng yí ge, 'taste one first', is the real invitation, and 尝 cháng means to taste. Saying 我不要钱 wǒ bú yào qián sounds like you're refusing payment yourself, which is confusing. Just taste it and give her the word she's hoping for: 真甜 zhēn tián.","tip":"不A不B means if not A, then not B: 不甜不要钱, no sweet, no pay."},
"fruit|好吃。":{"why":"好吃 hǎochī is a general 'tasty' and perfectly polite. But she set up the moment with 甜 tián, so echoing her word, 真甜 zhēn tián, 'really sweet', confirms her promise and makes her happy. Sellers love it when you back up their pitch, and it's a natural lead into buying: 真甜，来两斤 zhēn tián, lái liǎng jīn.","tip":"Echo the seller's word: she says 甜, you say 真甜."},
"fruit|少的也行。":{"why":"小 xiǎo and 少 shǎo are both third tone, so only the first sound tells them apart. For x, the tongue tip rests behind the lower teeth and the lips spread a little, like a smile; for sh, the tongue curls back. 少的 shǎo de sounds like 'the few ones', which doesn't quite mean anything here, though she'll likely guess.","tip":"小 small has a smiling x; 少 few has a curled sh."},
"fruit|我要大的。":{"why":"不多了 bù duō le is 'not many left', with 了 le marking that things have changed. 剩下的都是小的 shèngxià de dōu shì xiǎo de means 'what's left are all small', and the 行吗 xíng ma at the end asks for your OK. Insisting on the big ones she just said are running out sounds like you missed her point. If you really want big ones, 还有大的吗 hái yǒu dà de ma is the polite ask.","tip":"不多了 is running low; 没有了 is all gone."},
"fruit|加什么？":{"why":"加 jiā, 'add', is the everyday verb for connecting on WeChat: 加微信 jiā Wēixìn, 加个好友 jiā ge hǎoyǒu. Then you scan, 扫 sǎo: 我扫你 wǒ sǎo nǐ means 'I'll scan your code' and 你扫我 nǐ sǎo wǒ means 'you scan mine'. Plenty of small stalls take orders and deliver this way, so you'll hear 加个微信 constantly.","tip":"加 add, 扫 scan: 我扫你 is how a WeChat friendship starts."},
"fruit|不加。":{"why":"不 plus a bare verb is a flat refusal with no cushioning, so 不加 bù jiā sounds like a door closing. 不用了 bú yòng le, 'no need', frames it as not wanting to trouble her, and 谢谢 softens it further. Note 不 becomes bú before fourth-tone 用 yòng. Here, though, the task was to say yes: 好啊，我扫你 hǎo a, wǒ sǎo nǐ.","tip":"Decline with 不用了, not 不 plus a verb."},
"delivery|南门。":{"why":"Riders work on tight timers and will often leave the food and move on if they can't find you. 马上 mǎshàng, literally 'on horseback', means right away, and 下来 xiàlái means coming down toward the listener, so 我马上下来 wǒ mǎshàng xiàlái tells him 'wait, I'm on my way'. Putting the action first and the gate after is the natural phone rhythm.","tip":"马上 is 'on horseback': right away."},
"delivery|好的，我马上下来，北门。":{"why":"北 běi dips low in the third tone, while 南 nán rises. The four directions in their usual order are 东南西北 dōng nán xī běi, so you can run through them to check. Residential compounds, 小区 xiǎoqū, often have gates on several sides, and they can be a long walk apart.","tip":"Recite 东南西北: east, south, west, north."},
"delivery|八楼。":{"why":"号 hào means 'number', so 八号楼 bā hào lóu is building No. 8, while 八楼 bā lóu with no 号 is the eighth floor. He asked 几号楼 jǐ hào lóu, which is your clue to keep the 号. A full compound address usually stacks them: building, then entrance 单元 dānyuán, then flat, like 八号楼三单元802.","tip":"号 means number: 八号楼 is building 8; 八楼 is floor 8."},
"delivery|不要。":{"why":"不要 bú yào means 'don't want', and with a delivery the obvious 'it' is the food. Before a verb it's also a command, 'don't!', so it can sound sharp. 不用了 bú yòng le, 'no need', is how you turn down a favour, and 我下去拿 wǒ xiàqù ná, 'I'll go down and get it', explains why.","tip":"不要 refuses a thing; 不用 refuses a favour."},
"delivery|好吃！":{"why":"辛苦了 xīnkǔ le, literally 'it's been hard work', is the standard thanks for delivery riders, couriers and anyone who's run around for you. It acknowledges their effort, which matters in a job that's all rush and weather. 好吃 hǎochī is for after you've eaten, so it sounds a bit like a joke at the gate.","tip":"Anyone who carried something to you gets 辛苦了."},
"delivery|你进来吧。":{"why":"让 ràng means to let or allow, so 保安不让我进去 bǎo'ān bú ràng wǒ jìnqù is 'the guard won't let me in'. Many compounds don't allow riders inside, so this happens all the time. Direction words matter: 来 lái is toward the speaker and 去 qù is away, so he asked 你能出来吗 nǐ néng chūlái ma, 'can you come out', and you answer 我现在出来 wǒ xiànzài chūlái.","tip":"让 is let: 不让进 means no entry."},
"delivery|三千七百一十六。":{"why":"Phone numbers, room numbers and bus numbers are read digit by digit, never as one big number. In those strings 一 yī becomes 幺 yāo, so it can't be confused with 七 qī over a noisy line. 尾号 wěihào, 'tail number', is the last four digits that delivery apps show from your masked number.","tip":"In strings of digits, 1 is 幺 yāo so it can't sound like 七 qī."},
"delivery|好的，差评。":{"why":"别 bié means 'don't' and everything after it is what he's begging you not to do: 你别给差评啊 nǐ bié gěi chàpíng a. Replying 好的，差评 hǎo de, chàpíng picks up the scariest word in his sentence and agrees to it. Bad reviews can cost riders real money, so this is a genuine plea. 没关系 méi guānxi and 没事儿 méi shìr both mean 'it's fine'.","tip":"别 bié is don't: whatever follows it, he doesn't want."},
"delivery|不好意思。":{"why":"不好意思 bù hǎoyìsi is what he just said to apologise, so saying it back sounds like you're apologising too. The reply to an apology is reassurance: 没关系 méi guānxi, 'it doesn't matter', or the more casual 没事儿 méi shìr, 'no problem'. To fully set his mind at rest you could add 不会给差评的 bú huì gěi chàpíng de, 'I won't leave a bad review'.","tip":"Someone says 不好意思, you say 没事儿."},
"delivery|我要可乐。":{"why":"没有了 méiyǒu le means 'there's none left', with 了 marking that it ran out. 换成 huànchéng means 'swapped into', so the restaurant has already put Sprite in the bag. The rider only carries what the shop packed, so insisting on Coke with him gets you nowhere. 雪碧也行 Xuěbì yě xíng, 'Sprite works too', closes it neatly.","tip":"X也行 is the easy way to say 'X is fine too'."},
"delivery|我不要。":{"why":"我不要 wǒ bú yào with no object leaves 'it' open, and the biggest 'it' in the conversation is the whole order. If you mind the swap, the place to raise it is the app, by contacting the shop, 联系商家 liánxì shāngjiā. Naming the drink, 雪碧也行 Xuěbì yě xíng, plus 没事 méi shì, keeps it about one can and reassures the rider.","tip":"Name the thing: 雪碧也行 keeps it about the drink, not the order."},
"train|杭州。|Fine at a busy w":{"why":"A ticket clerk needs three things: how many, where and when. 一张去杭州的票 yì zhāng qù Hángzhōu de piào packs the first two into one phrase: 张 zhāng is the measure word for flat things like tickets, and everything before 的 describes the 票. Tacking 今天的 jīntiān de on the end as an afterthought is exactly how locals do it.","tip":"Number, place, date: 一张, 去杭州, 今天的."},
"train|一张去杭州的票，明天的。":{"why":"The day words share a pattern: 昨天 zuótiān, 今天 jīntiān, 明天 míngtiān, yesterday, today, tomorrow. 今 jīn means 'now', which is also why this year is 今年 jīnnián. Ticket windows sell days ahead, so she would happily print you a ticket for tomorrow without blinking.","tip":"今 jīn is now: 今天 today, 今年 this year."},
"train|十点三分可以。":{"why":"Chinese clock time runs big to small, like dates: hour with 点 diǎn, then minutes with 分 fēn. So 三点十分 sān diǎn shí fēn is 3:10, and 十点三分 is 10:03, which people often say as 十点零三分 shí diǎn líng sān fēn. Whatever number sits next to 点 is the hour.","tip":"The number before 点 is the hour; the number before 分 is the minutes."},
"train|随便。":{"why":"随便 suíbiàn means 'as you like', and to a busy clerk it can sound like you can't be bothered. It also skips the choice she offered: 高铁 gāotiě (G trains, fastest) or 动车 dòngchē (D trains, a bit slower and cheaper). 都可以 dōu kěyǐ is friendlier, but since you want the high-speed one, the clearest answer is 三点十分可以，高铁 sān diǎn shí fēn kěyǐ, gāotiě.","tip":"G is 高铁 gāotiě, the fast one: 高 means high."},
"train|不要护照。":{"why":"Chinese train tickets are real-name, 实名制 shímíngzhì, so the clerk has to type your passport number in. These days the ticket is usually electronic and your passport literally is your ticket at the gate. 不要护照 bú yào hùzhào sounds like 'don't want passport', which makes no sense to her. When handing something over, 给你 gěi nǐ, 'here you go', is all you need.","tip":"In China, your passport is your train ticket."},
"train|好，一等座。":{"why":"Seats come in classes: 二等座 èrděngzuò (second), 一等座 yīděngzuò (first) and 商务座 shāngwùzuò (business), and first class costs noticeably more than second. Note 一 stays yī in 一等 because it means 'first', an order, so no tone change. 一等座多少钱 yīděngzuò duōshao qián lets you decide before you're committed.","tip":"Before you say 好, ask 多少钱."},
"train|那我站着。":{"why":"那我站着 nà wǒ zhànzhe is understandable, with 着 zhe marking an ongoing state, 'I'll be standing'. But at the window the thing you buy is a 无座 wú zuò ticket, 'no seat', so 有无座的吗 yǒu wú zuò de ma is how to ask. The task was to find out the first-class price, though, so 一等座多少钱 yīděngzuò duōshao qián comes first.","tip":"无座 wú zuò, no seat, is the name of a standing ticket."},
"train|要。":{"why":"要…吗 yào…ma questions are answered by echoing 要 for yes, so you just agreed to a return ticket. 往返 wǎngfǎn is built from 往 wǎng (going) and 返 fǎn (coming back). The opposite is 单程 dānchéng, 单 single plus 程 journey. When a word flies past, 什么 shénme or 什么意思 shénme yìsi stops you buying something you don't want.","tip":"往 out plus 返 back is a return; 单 single plus 程 journey is one way."},
"train|一张就行。":{"why":"一张 yì zhāng answers 'how many', not 'which direction', so she had to guess that you meant no return. 单程 dānchéng, one way, is the exact word for this. 就行 jiù xíng, 'that'll do', is a handy ending for any 'just this, thanks' moment.","tip":"单 dān is single: 单程 is a single journey."},
"train|杭州。|She was asking w":{"why":"A question with 还是 háishi is an either-or, so the answer is one of the two options. 杭州东 Hángzhōu Dōng and 杭州站 Hángzhōu Zhàn are different stations, and East is the big high-speed hub. Lots of big cities have several main stations, like 上海虹桥 Shànghǎi Hóngqiáo and 上海站 Shànghǎi Zhàn, so this question comes up often.","tip":"When you hear 还是, pick a side."},
"train|是的。":{"why":"In questions, 还是 háishi means 'or' and asks you to choose, so 是的 shì de, 'yes', doesn't answer it. In statements, 'or' is a different word, 或者 huòzhě. So when you hear A 还是 B with a rising question feel, reply with A or B.","tip":"还是 asks 'which one', so the answer is never 是."},
"train|人口通道在哪儿？":{"why":"工 gōng means work or labour, so 人工 réngōng is 'done by people', staffed, while 口 kǒu is mouth, and 人口 rénkǒu, 'people mouths', is population. 通道 tōngdào is a lane or passage. Foreign passports don't always work at the face-scan gates, so the 人工通道 is where a person checks you. She'll understand you, but the right word saves a puzzled look.","tip":"工 gōng is work: 人工 is work done by a person."},
"train|好的，没问题。":{"why":"提醒你 tíxǐng nǐ, 'just a reminder', flags that what follows is important. 走 zǒu here means 'go through', and 人工通道 réngōng tōngdào is the staffed lane. Nodding along means you'll find out at the automatic gates when your passport won't scan. 人工通道在哪儿 réngōng tōngdào zài nǎr checks the word and gets directions in one go.","tip":"Agreeing to a word you don't know is how you end up in the wrong queue."},
"return|太小了。":{"why":"Mandarin likes to set the scene before the comment: topic first, then what happened. 这件衣服我昨天买的 zhè jiàn yīfu wǒ zuótiān mǎi de, 'this shirt, I bought yesterday', uses 的 at the end to stress when, the trimmed form of a 是…的 sentence. 件 jiàn is the measure word for clothes. With that context, 太小了 tài xiǎo le tells her straight away this is a return.","tip":"Topic first: 这件衣服, then the story."},
"return|这件衣服太少了。":{"why":"Mandarin keeps size and amount separate: 大 dà and 小 xiǎo are size, 多 duō and 少 shǎo are amount. In fact the words for 'size' and 'how many' are built from those pairs: 大小 dàxiǎo and 多少 duōshao. 太少了 tài shǎo le sounds like 'too few' or 'too little', odd for one shirt. 小 x has the tongue flat behind the lower teeth; 少 sh curls it back.","tip":"Size is 大小 dàxiǎo; amount is 多少 duōshao."},
"return|我要退钱。":{"why":"退 tuì is to return something for your money back, and 换 huàn is to swap it. 可以换，不能退 kěyǐ huàn, bù néng tuì is a very common shop policy, often printed on the receipt. 大一号 dà yí hào, 'one size bigger', has 一 as yí because 号 hào is fourth tone.","tip":"退 tuì is money back; 换 huàn is a swap."},
"return|好，换小一号的。":{"why":"Adjective plus 一号 yí hào means 'one size more' or 'one size less': 大一号 dà yí hào is a size up and 小一号 xiǎo yí hào a size down. She actually said 大一号 in her own question, so echoing her words back is the safest way to answer. 换小一号的 would get you an even tighter shirt.","tip":"Too 小? Go 大一号."},
"return|这件太大了。":{"why":"正好 zhènghǎo means 'just right', and 合适 héshì, 'suits, fits well', also works. 太大了 tài dà le would send her looking for yet another size. 试一下 shì yíxià, 'try it', and 试衣间 shìyījiān, 'try-clothes room', are words you'll hear in every clothes shop.","tip":"正好 zhènghǎo: not too big, not too small, just right."},
"return|没带。":{"why":"小票 xiǎopiào, literally 'small ticket', is the till receipt, the little slip you got when you paid. Questions ending in 带了吗 dài le ma are answered by echoing the verb: 带了 for yes, 没带 méi dài for no. So 没带 told her you came without it, the opposite of what's in your hand. 带了，给你 dài le, gěi nǐ answers and hands it over in one go.","tip":"小票 is the little slip: answer 带了 and hand it over."},
"return|什么票？":{"why":"Asking 什么票 shénme piào shows the word you did catch, which makes it easy for her to fill in the rest. 小票 xiǎopiào is the ordinary shop receipt you need for returns. 发票 fāpiào is the official tax invoice people request so they can claim expenses, 报销 bàoxiāo, and you usually have to ask for it specially.","tip":"小票 for returns, 发票 for expense claims."},
"return|我要这个颜色。":{"why":"Verb plus 完 wán means 'done until there's none left', so 卖完了 mài wán le is sold out, just as 吃完了 chī wán le is eaten up. She's already told you this colour is gone in your size, so insisting on it goes nowhere. 换个颜色 huàn ge yánsè, 'switch colour', is her offer, and 有什么颜色 yǒu shénme yánsè picks it up.","tip":"Verb plus 完了 means all done: 卖完了 sold out."},
"return|有什么颜色的大一号？":{"why":"In Mandarin, descriptions with 的 come before the noun, and the thing you're talking about tends to go first as a topic. A natural version is 大一号的有什么颜色 dà yí hào de yǒu shénme yánsè, 'the size-up ones, what colours are there?'. Since she just said the size-up is the issue, plain 有什么颜色 is enough.","tip":"Topic first, question last: 大一号的有什么颜色?"},
"return|不要！":{"why":"打八折 dǎ bā zhé means you pay eight tenths of the price, so 20% off: Chinese discounts name what you pay, not what you save. A bare 不要 bú yào is a hard refusal, and after she's just sorted your exchange it sounds curt. 不用了，谢谢 bú yòng le, xièxie, maybe plus 下次吧 xià cì ba, 'next time', closes it warmly.","tip":"八折 means pay eight tenths: the number is what you pay."},
"cafe|一个冰拿铁。":{"why":"Measure words match the shape or container of a thing, and drinks are counted in cups: 杯 bēi. 个 ge is the all-purpose fallback, and it gets you understood, but it sounds a little like a beginner. Note 一杯 is yì bēi, because 杯 is first tone. 拿铁 nátiě is a sound-borrowing of 'latte'.","tip":"Drinks come in cups: 一杯 for anything you sip."},
"cafe|一杯病拿铁。":{"why":"冰 bīng is first tone, high and level, while 病 bìng is fourth tone, a sharp fall. Same syllable, different tone, completely different word, so 病拿铁 sounds like a sick latte. The barista will likely guess, but holding 冰 high and steady is the fix. You'll also need 冰的 bīng de, iced, and 热的 rè de, hot.","tip":"Ice stays cold and level: 冰 bīng holds high."},
"cafe|还是。":{"why":"还是 háishi is the 'or' in an either-or question, so saying it back is like answering 'or'. Just pick a size: 中杯 zhōng bēi. In Starbucks-style chains 中杯 is a Tall, 大杯 dà bēi a Grande and 超大杯 chāo dà bēi a Venti, so the sizes run one bigger than you'd expect.","tip":"Pick a side of the 还是: 中杯."},
"cafe|有Wi-Fi吗？":{"why":"Nearly every café has wifi, so 有Wi-Fi吗 yǒu Wi-Fi ma gets a quick 有 and then you still have to ask for the password. 密码 mìmǎ, 'secret code', is the word for any password. Codes and numbers are asked with 多少 duōshao, not 什么, so 密码是多少 mìmǎ shì duōshao. It's often printed on the receipt or a sign by the till.","tip":"Codes and numbers take 多少: 密码是多少?"},
"cafe|Wi-Fi的名字是什么？":{"why":"The network name usually shows up on your phone anyway, often just the café's name. The bit you can't guess is 密码 mìmǎ, the password. Also, names are normally asked with 叫什么 jiào shénme, 'called what', so Wi-Fi叫什么 sounds more natural than 名字是什么.","tip":"The name is on your screen; the 密码 is what you ask for."},
"cafe|不加汤，谢谢。":{"why":"糖 táng is second tone and rises, while 汤 tāng is first tone and stays high and flat. A café has no soup, so the barista will probably work it out, but it's a funny moment. 加 jiā means to add, so 不加糖 bù jiā táng is 'don't add sugar'.","tip":"Sugar rises sweetly: 糖 táng goes up."},
"cafe|要。":{"why":"Echoing 要 yào answers 'yes', so you'll get sugar in your coffee. For no, put 不 in front of her verb: 不加糖 bù jiā táng, or 不要糖 bú yào táng. In milk tea shops you'll also be asked about sweetness levels, where 少糖 shǎo táng is less sugar and 无糖 wú táng is none.","tip":"不加 means leave it out: 不加糖, 不加冰."},
"cafe|那就冰的吧。":{"why":"制冰机 zhìbīngjī is the 'make-ice machine', and 坏了 huài le means it's broken. The key word is 只有 zhǐ yǒu, 'only have', so 只有热的 means hot is the only choice. Your 那就…吧 nà jiù…ba, 'then I'll go with', is exactly right; just put 热的 rè de in the middle.","tip":"只有 zhǐ yǒu means only: what follows is your only choice."},
"cafe|是。":{"why":"Saying 是 shì means you're a 会员 huìyuán, so the next question is your 手机号 shǒujīhào, phone number, to find your account, and you'd be stuck. Answering a 是…吗 question with no is just 不是 bú shì, with 不 rising before fourth-tone 是. If you're not sure what she asked, 不是 is the safe answer here.","tip":"Not a member? Just 不是, and you're done."},
"cafe|会员是什么？":{"why":"员 yuán means a person in a role, as in 服务员 fúwùyuán (waiter) and 店员 diànyuán (shop assistant), so 会员 huìyuán is a member of a club or loyalty scheme. Chains usually sign you up with your phone number or a mini-program for points and discounts. If you're not interested, 不是，谢谢 bú shì, xièxie is enough.","tip":"员 yuán means a person in a role: 会员 member, 服务员 waiter."},
"cafe|我坐窗户。":{"why":"坐 zuò can be followed by a place word like 那边 nàbiān, but 窗户 chuānghu is an object, the glass itself, so 坐窗户 sounds like sitting on the window. You need a position word to turn it into a place: 窗户旁边 chuānghu pángbiān. The set phrase for a window seat is 靠窗 kào chuāng, 'against the window', which is what she said.","tip":"You sit by a window, not on it: 靠窗 or 窗户旁边."},
"cafe|插座是什么？":{"why":"插 chā means to insert or plug in, and 座 zuò is a seat or base, so 插座 chāzuò is where your plug sits. The plug on your cable is 插头 chātóu. Related words worth knowing are 充电 chōngdiàn, to charge, and 充电宝 chōngdiànbǎo, a power bank, which you can rent in most cafés.","tip":"插 is plug in, 座 is a seat: the seat your plug sits in."},
"bike|喂！帮我！":{"why":"喂 wèi is 'hello' on the phone, but shouted at a stranger it's a blunt 'hey, you!'. A bare 帮我 bāng wǒ with no softener sounds urgent, close to crying for help. 能…一下吗 néng…yíxià ma turns it into a small favour, with 一下 yíxià meaning 'just briefly'. 大爷 dàye is a warm, respectful way to address an older man, especially in the north.","tip":"一下 makes any request small: 帮我一下."},
"bike|不开。":{"why":"不开 bù kāi on its own sounds like a refusal, as if the bike or you won't open on purpose. Chinese builds \"can't manage to\" with a potential complement: action verb + 不 + result, so 打不开 dǎ bu kāi means you tried the action and the result \"open\" didn't happen. The middle 不 goes light and toneless in fast speech, which is why you hear dǎ bu kāi. Starting with 我扫了码 wǒ sǎo le mǎ also tells him you did the right step, so he knows to look at the bike.","tip":"Can't open is a sandwich: 打 + 不 + 开, try + not + result."},
"bike|我扫了地，可是打不开。":{"why":"扫 sǎo is literally a sweeping motion, and the QR-code meaning grew out of the phone sweeping across the code, so the verb is the same and only the object changes. 扫地 sǎo dì (sweep the floor) is one of the first chores kids learn, so the old man hears a cleaning story, not a bike problem. Swap in 码 mǎ, short for 二维码 èrwéimǎ (QR code), and 扫码 sǎo mǎ becomes the everyday word you see on every bike, menu and shop counter.","tip":"地 dì is the ground you sweep; 码 mǎ is the code you scan."},
"bike|你的车坏了。":{"why":"He already spotted the problem and offered advice, so repeating that the bike is broken just hands his own news back to him, and 你的车 nǐ de chē wrongly suggests it's his bike. 这辆 zhè liàng uses 辆 liàng, the measure word for vehicles, meaning \"this one\" you just picked. When a stranger helps, the natural move is thanks, and 大爷 dàye (uncle, for an older man) adds warmth that locals really appreciate.","tip":"When someone helps, thank first: 谢谢大爷 xièxie dàye."},
"bike|开了。":{"why":"了 le marks that a change has happened, so 开了 kāi le reports \"it's switched on\" and he'll assume Bluetooth isn't the problem. To say something hasn't happened, Chinese uses 没 méi, not 不, so 没开 méi kāi means \"not switched on (yet)\" and drops the 了. Then 我现在开 wǒ xiànzài kāi, I'll turn it on now, tells him you're fixing it.","tip":"Done is 开了; not done is 没开, with no 了."},
"bike|蓝牙是什么？":{"why":"蓝牙 lányá is a literal translation of Bluetooth: 蓝 lán is blue and 牙 yá is tooth. Asking is perfectly fine, he'll just explain or point to your phone. Once you know, answer his yes/no question with 没开 méi kāi (not on) and promise to switch it on with 我现在开 wǒ xiànzài kāi.","tip":"Blue + tooth = 蓝牙 lányá, word for word."},
"bike|我是大学。":{"why":"是 shì links two things that are the same, so 我是大学 wǒ shì dàxué says you are a university building. 学生 xuésheng (student; the second syllable is usually toneless) is the person, and 大学生 dàxuéshēng is a university student. He actually asked about 留学生 liúxuéshēng, a student from abroad, so 对 duì (yes) plus what you're doing here answers him naturally.","tip":"Add 生 shēng (person who's learning) to make the place into a person: 大学 to 大学生."},
"bike|对，我学中文在这儿。":{"why":"English puts the place at the end (I study Chinese here), but Chinese sets the scene first: who, where, then the action, 我在这儿学中文 wǒ zài zhèr xué Zhōngwén. Here 在 zài works like \"at\" and the whole 在这儿 phrase must sit before the verb. The ending 在这儿 after a verb is reserved for where something ends up, as in 放在这儿 fàng zài zhèr (put it here), so your version sounds foreign even though it's clear.","tip":"Set the stage before the action: 在这儿 comes before 学."},
"bike|停在白线外面？":{"why":"里 lǐ and 外 wài are a pair, and 面 miàn just means \"side\", so 里面 lǐmiàn is the inside and 外面 wàimiàn the outside. Getting this backwards matters: bike apps fine you or keep charging if you park outside the painted zone. When checking, repeat his own words and add 对吧 duì ba, which asks \"that's right, isn't it?\".","tip":"里 lǐ is in, like the \"li\" in \"lining\"; 外 wài is out, like a \"way out\"."},
"bike|罚多少钱？":{"why":"罚 fá (to fine) is a useful word, but the task was to confirm where to park, and he'll hear you skipping the instruction to ask about money. 不然 bùrán means \"otherwise\", so the fine is just the warning; the key info is 白线里面 bái xiàn lǐmiàn, inside the white lines. Repeat that part back with 对吧 duì ba and you've confirmed it.","tip":"Echo the instruction, not the threat: 白线里面，对吧？"},
"clinic|我兔子疼。":{"why":"Mandarin d and t are both made in the same spot; the only difference is that t comes with a puff of air and d doesn't. Let a puff of air slip out on d and it turns into t, and 兔子 tùzi (rabbit) is the result. Also 肚子 dùzi has a falling fourth tone on the first syllable and a light, toneless 子. Try saying it with a hand in front of your mouth: you shouldn't feel air on 肚.","tip":"No puff for 肚 dù: hold a hand to your lips and keep it still."},
"clinic|肚子。":{"why":"肚子 dùzi alone names a body part but not the problem, so the nurse has to guess whether it hurts, is upset, or something else. 疼 téng (hurt, ache) after the body part gives the complete Chinese pattern: body part + 疼, like 头疼 tóuténg (headache). With 我肚子疼 wǒ dùzi téng she can pick the department straight away.","tip":"Body part + 疼 téng = it hurts there."},
"clinic|有。":{"why":"有 yǒu is a yes; the only negative of 有 is 没有 méiyǒu, never 不有. Saying 有 means she'll wait for you to hand a card over. 医保卡 yībǎo kǎ is China's medical insurance card, and as a foreigner without it you pay yourself, 自费 zìfèi, literally \"self-expense\".","tip":"有 always flips with 没: 没有 méiyǒu, never 不有."},
"clinic|三号？":{"why":"楼 lóu means a building, and with a number before it, a floor: 三楼 sān lóu is the third floor. 号 hào is a number in a sequence, like a queue ticket or a date, so 三号 sān hào sounds like ticket three or the 3rd of the month. In a hospital you'll hear both: go to a 楼 floor, then wait for your 号 number to be called, 叫号 jiào hào.","tip":"楼 lóu is up the stairs; 号 hào is on your ticket."},
"clinic|好的。":{"why":"好的 hǎo de is a perfectly polite \"OK\", but Chinese hospitals are loud and fast, and staff rattle off price, floor and next steps in one breath. Repeating the key bits, 三楼 sān lóu and 等叫号 děng jiào hào, and adding 对吗 duì ma lets the nurse correct you on the spot. Nurses are used to this and won't find it annoying.","tip":"In a hospital, echo the floor and the step back with 对吗."},
"clinic|我没有身份证。":{"why":"身份证 shēnfènzhèng is the Chinese national ID card, which only citizens have, so staff use the word by habit. 或者 huòzhě means \"or\", so she already offered the passport, 护照 hùzhào, as the other choice. Telling her what you don't have makes her repeat herself; 这是我的护照 zhè shì wǒ de hùzhào while handing it over is all she needs.","tip":"Hear 或者 huòzhě? Pick the option that fits and hand it over."},
"clinic|我不看。":{"why":"给我看一下 gěi wǒ kàn yíxià is literally \"give me to look a bit\": 给我 gěi wǒ means for me, so she is the one doing the looking. 一下 yíxià softens a request, like \"just a sec\". Answering 我不看 wǒ bú kàn (I won't look) sounds like you're refusing, so hand it over with 这是我的护照 zhè shì wǒ de hùzhào.","tip":"给我看 = let ME look; the 我 after 给 is the looker."},
"clinic|没有。":{"why":"She asked two yes/no questions in a row, and a single 没有 méiyǒu sounds like no to both, which hides the symptom that matters. In Chinese you usually answer by repeating the verb: 不发烧 bù fāshāo (no fever) and 拉肚子 lā dùzi (yes, diarrhoea). 但是 dànshì (but) links the two halves clearly.","tip":"Two questions, two answers: repeat each verb with or without 不."},
"clinic|发烧。":{"why":"In Chinese, yes is often given by repeating the verb, so a bare 发烧 fāshāo is heard as \"yes, I have a fever\". That has real consequences: Chinese hospitals send feverish patients to a separate 发热门诊 fārè ménzhěn (fever clinic). Put 不 bù in front, 不发烧 bù fāshāo, then add 但是拉肚子 dànshì lā dùzi.","tip":"Repeating a verb says yes; only 不 in front makes it no."},
"clinic|不用。":{"why":"不用 bú yòng means \"no need\", which is correct but bare, like a curt \"don't need\". Adding 了 le, 不用了 bú yòng le, softens it into \"oh, that's OK\", and 谢谢 xièxie shows you appreciate the offer. 说中文就行 shuō Zhōngwén jiù xíng (Chinese is fine) explains why, so she doesn't think you're refusing help out of pride.","tip":"Decline softly: 不用了 + 谢谢, never 不用 alone."},
"clinic|我是英语。":{"why":"是 shì works like an equals sign between two nouns, so 我是英语 wǒ shì Yīngyǔ means \"I = the English language\". 语 yǔ marks a language and 人 rén marks a person, so a native speaker would be 英国人 Yīngguó rén or 美国人 Měiguó rén. But the task was to decline her offer of help: 不用了，谢谢 bú yòng le, xièxie, then 说中文就行 shuō Zhōngwén jiù xíng.","tip":"语 yǔ is a language, 人 rén is a person; you can only 是 a person."},
"clinic|行。":{"why":"行 xíng (OK) accepts the afternoon slot, which is fine, but you'll leave without knowing when to come back. 几点 jǐ diǎn literally asks \"how many dots on the clock\", the standard way to ask what time. 下午几点 xiàwǔ jǐ diǎn is short, polite enough, and the nurse expects it.","tip":"Accept, then pin the time: 下午几点？"},
"clinic|我要上午的。":{"why":"完 wán after a verb means the action is finished or used up, so 挂完了 guà wán le is \"the registrations are used up\". You'll hear this pattern everywhere: 卖完了 mài wán le (sold out), 吃完了 chī wán le (finished eating). Asking for the morning again won't help; ask 下午几点 xiàwǔ jǐ diǎn instead.","tip":"Verb + 完了 = all gone, nothing left."},
"dumplings|一两是什么？":{"why":"两 liǎng is an old weight unit, 50 grams, and northern dumpling houses sell dumplings by weight rather than by piece. 一两是什么 yì liǎng shì shénme asks for a definition, which the waitress may answer with grams; 一两有几个 yì liǎng yǒu jǐ ge asks how many pieces, which is what you actually need to order. 几 jǐ is the question word for small numbers you expect to be under ten or so.","tip":"Ask 有几个 to turn weight into dumplings."},
"dumplings|要一两个。":{"why":"两 liǎng has two lives: as a number it means two, and as a measure it's a 50-gram weight. 一两个 yì liǎng ge, with 个 ge after it, can only be the number reading, and two numbers side by side mean \"one or two\", like 三四个 sān sì ge (three or four). On a dumpling menu, 两 is the weight, so ask 一两有几个 yì liǎng yǒu jǐ ge.","tip":"两 followed by 个 is a number; 两 standing alone on a menu is a weight."},
"dumplings|要三斤猪肉白菜的。":{"why":"斤 jīn is 500 grams and 两 liǎng is 50 grams, so three 斤 of dumplings would be around 180 pieces. You'll hear 斤 at markets for fruit and meat; 两 is the unit for dumplings, noodles and rice in canteens. The waitress would probably laugh and double-check, so say 三两 sān liǎng.","tip":"斤 jīn feeds a family; 两 liǎng feeds you."},
"dumplings|要猪肉白菜的。":{"why":"猪肉白菜的 zhūròu báicài de names the filling, with 的 de standing in for \"the ones\", but the order isn't complete without an amount. In dumpling houses the amount comes before the filling: number + 两 liǎng + filling + 的. So 要三两猪肉白菜的 yào sān liǎng zhūròu báicài de is the whole order in one go.","tip":"Order shape: 要 + how much + which filling + 的."},
"dumplings|睡觉，谢谢。":{"why":"水饺 shuǐjiǎo is two third tones together, and by tone sandhi the first rises, so it actually sounds like shuíjiǎo, rising then dipping. 睡觉 shuìjiào is two sharp falling tones. Also listen to the vowels: jiǎo and jiào are the same syllable, so tone is the whole difference. Think of 水 shuǐ (water) as the clue: boiled dumplings are water dumplings, and 煎饺 jiānjiǎo are fried ones.","tip":"Water dumplings rise and dip, shuíjiǎo; sleep crashes down twice, shuìjiào."},
"dumplings|要三两韭菜。":{"why":"韭菜 jiǔcài is just the chives themselves, so 三两韭菜 sounds like 150 grams of the vegetable. The 的 de at the end of 猪肉韭菜的 zhūròu jiǔcài de is what makes it \"the pork-and-chive ones\", meaning the dumplings. She'll likely understand, but the 的 is what dumpling-house regulars say.","tip":"Filling + 的 = the dumplings with that filling."},
"dumplings|好，三两猪肉白菜的。":{"why":"刚 gāng means \"just now\" and 卖完 mài wán means sold out, so 刚卖完 gāng mài wán is \"just sold out\". Ordering the same thing again tells her you missed it. When someone offers a swap with 行吗 xíng ma, accept with 行 xíng and 那 nà (then): 行，那要三两猪肉韭菜的 xíng, nà yào sān liǎng zhūròu jiǔcài de.","tip":"Hear 卖完 mài wán? That dish is gone, pick the offer."},
"dumplings|饺子很够。":{"why":"够 gòu is about reaching a limit, so Chinese marks it as a state you've arrived at with 了 le: 够了 gòu le, that's enough. 很够 hěn gòu isn't how people say it. 就 jiù adds \"already, just\", so 饺子就够了 jiǎozi jiù gòu le is \"the dumplings are already enough\", and 不用了 bú yòng le in front makes it a polite no thanks.","tip":"Enough is a finish line: 够了, not 很够."},
"dumplings|好的，谢谢。":{"why":"要不要 yào bu yào (want or not) is how Chinese makes an offer, and 好的 hǎo de means \"OK, sure\", so she'll bring the smashed cucumber, 拍黄瓜 pāi huánggua. 谢谢 xièxie alone doesn't decline in Chinese the way \"no thanks\" does in English. Lead with 不用了 bú yòng le, then give your reason.","tip":"To an offer, 好的 = yes; 不用了 = no thanks."},
"dumplings|我一个人。":{"why":"She can see you're alone; that's exactly why she's asking you to share. 拼 pīn means to piece together, so 拼桌 pīn zhuō is sharing a table with strangers, completely normal at busy, cheap restaurants in China. 行吗 xíng ma is asking for permission, so 行 xíng or 没问题 méi wèntí (no problem) answers it.","tip":"Asked with 行吗？ Reply with 行."},
"dumplings|不要。":{"why":"拼桌 pīn zhuō is a normal part of eating out in busy Chinese places, and nobody expects you to talk to the other diner. 不要 bú yào is a flat \"don't want\" and sounds childish or rude to a request, especially with the man standing right there. If you really didn't want to, people say something soft like 不太方便 bú tài fāngbiàn, but here the task is to agree: 行，没问题 xíng, méi wèntí.","tip":"拼桌 is normal; answer 行，没问题."},
"landlord|我家没有水。":{"why":"没有水 méiyǒu shuǐ means no water at all, and a landlord will think of a burst pipe or water cut. 热水 rèshuǐ (hot water) is one word in Chinese, used for showers and kettles alike. Adding 了 le at the end, 没有热水了 méiyǒu rèshuǐ le, says it was working before and has now stopped, which is exactly the situation.","tip":"Be specific: 热水 rèshuǐ, and add 了 for \"it's stopped\"."},
"landlord|我家太热了。":{"why":"太热了 tài rè le is about temperature in the room, so the landlord hears \"it's too hot in my flat\", maybe air-con trouble. 热 rè (hot) only becomes hot water when joined with 水 shuǐ: 热水 rèshuǐ. Then 没有热水了 méiyǒu rèshuǐ le, no more hot water.","tip":"热 is the weather; 热水 is the shower."},
"landlord|开了。":{"why":"开了 kāi le stresses a change, so after \"check the switch\" it sounds like you just switched it on and the problem is fixed. 着 zhe marks an ongoing state, and 呢 ne adds \"actually, it is\", so 开着呢 kāizhe ne means \"it's on, and has been\". Then 可是不热 kěshì bú rè, but it isn't hot, keeps him working on the problem.","tip":"开了 = just switched on; 开着呢 = been on all along."},
"landlord|好的，谢谢。":{"why":"好的，谢谢 hǎo de, xièxie is polite, but the repairman, 师傅 shīfu, may turn up while you're out. 几点 jǐ diǎn asks what time; putting 明天 míngtiān in front, 明天几点 míngtiān jǐ diǎn, pins it to tomorrow. Time words come first in a Chinese question.","tip":"Thanks, then pin it down: 明天几点？"},
"landlord|五号楼一千二百零三。":{"why":"Chinese reads room, flat, phone and code numbers digit by digit, like reading a label, and saves 一千二百 yìqiān èrbǎi for real quantities. In those strings 一 is said yāo, so it can't be mixed up with 七 qī, giving 一二零三 yāo èr líng sān. 零 líng (zero) is always said. People also say 十二零三 shí'èr líng sān, floor 12 then room 03.","tip":"Addresses are labels, not amounts: read them digit by digit with yāo for 1."},
"landlord|五楼一二零三。":{"why":"In a 小区 xiǎoqū (housing compound), 号楼 hào lóu means building number, so 五号楼 wǔ hào lóu is Building No. 5. Without 号, 五楼 wǔ lóu is the fifth floor, which clashes with 1203 being on the twelfth floor. Keep 号: number + 号 + 楼 for the building, then the flat number digit by digit.","tip":"号楼 = which building; 楼 alone = which floor."},
"landlord|交什么？":{"why":"燃气费 ránqì fèi is the gas bill: 燃 rán means burn, 气 qì is gas, and 费 fèi is a fee, just like 水费 shuǐ fèi (water bill) and 电费 diànfèi (electricity). Many Chinese flats use prepaid gas, so when credit runs out the heater stops. Asking is fine, then answer with 交了 jiāo le (paid), and 还有钱 hái yǒu qián (there's still credit).","tip":"Anything + 费 fèi is a bill: 燃气费 is the gas one."},
"landlord|我交了水费。":{"why":"水费 shuǐ fèi is the water bill, but many Chinese water heaters run on gas, 燃气 ránqì, so the landlord asked about the gas bill specifically. Answering about water makes him think the gas may still be unpaid. Just answer what he asked: 交了 jiāo le (paid), 还有钱 hái yǒu qián (still credit), where 还 hái means \"still\".","tip":"Hot water burns gas: 燃 rán, burn."},
"landlord|好，明天上午见。":{"why":"明天 míngtiān is tomorrow and 后天 hòutiān is the day after; 后 hòu means after or behind. The full set runs 前天 qiántiān, 昨天 zuótiān, 今天 jīntiān, 明天 míngtiān, 后天 hòutiān. He just said the repairman 明天休息 míngtiān xiūxi, takes tomorrow off, so agreeing to tomorrow means nobody shows up.","tip":"后 hòu is after: 后天 is the day after 明天."},
"wechat|你的电话号码是多少？":{"why":"In China nearly everyone connects on 微信 Wēixìn (WeChat) rather than swapping phone numbers, and asking for a number can feel more formal or even a bit odd. 加 jiā means add, and 个 ge in 加个微信 jiā ge Wēixìn makes it light and casual, like \"add a quick WeChat\". 吧 ba at the end turns it into a friendly suggestion. And don't skip 不客气 bú kèqi or 没事 méi shì first, since they thanked you.","tip":"In China you don't swap numbers, you 加个微信."},
"wechat|不客气！你家在哪儿？":{"why":"不客气 bú kèqi was a good start, but 你家在哪儿 nǐ jiā zài nǎr (where's your home) is personal for someone you just lent a pen to, and could feel nosy. Between new classmates, the low-pressure next step is WeChat: 我们加个微信吧 wǒmen jiā ge Wēixìn ba. 我们 wǒmen (we) frames it as something you do together.","tip":"Pen to WeChat is one step; pen to their home is three."},
"wechat|我扫地吧。":{"why":"扫 sǎo started as sweeping, and phones \"sweep\" codes, so the verb covers both. What changes the meaning is the object: 扫地 sǎo dì (sweep the floor) versus 扫你 sǎo nǐ (scan you, meaning your QR code). In WeChat talk people say 我扫你 wǒ sǎo nǐ, \"I scan you\", leaving out the word for code entirely, and 吧 ba makes it an offer.","tip":"In WeChat-speak you scan the person: 我扫你吧."},
"wechat|我是李明。":{"why":"Copying a sentence pattern is a great strategy, but you have to swap out their details for yours. 叫 jiào (to be called) is the natural verb for names; 是 shì works too, but 我是李明 wǒ shì Lǐ Míng still says you are Li Ming. Use 我叫 wǒ jiào + your Chinese name, and add 以后常联系 yǐhòu cháng liánxì (let's keep in touch) for the second half of the task.","tip":"Borrow the frame, swap the name: 我叫 + you."},
"wechat|那说我的手机号吧。":{"why":"s and sh differ in tongue position: for s the tongue tip rests behind the lower teeth, flat; for sh it curls back toward the roof of the mouth. 搜 sōu (search) and 说 shuō (say) also differ in their vowels, ou versus uo. In WeChat you 搜 sōu a phone number to find someone; 说我的手机号 shuō wǒ de shǒujī hào sounds like you want them to recite your number.","tip":"搜 sōu with a flat tongue, like the s in \"soul\"."},
"wechat|我要火锅。":{"why":"要 yào is \"want\" or \"I'll have\", so 我要火锅 wǒ yào huǒguō sounds like ordering hotpot from a waiter. The invitation was 你要不要一起来 nǐ yào bu yào yìqǐ lái, will you come along, so you accept the coming, not the food: 好啊 hǎo a or 我去 wǒ qù. Then add 几点 jǐ diǎn to ask the time.","tip":"Invited? Say 好啊, not 我要."},
"wechat|好啊！几天？":{"why":"几 jǐ means \"how many\", and the word after it decides what you're counting. 几天 jǐ tiān counts days, how many days; 几点 jǐ diǎn counts o'clock dots, what time. 点 diǎn originally meant the strokes of a clock, so whenever you ask about time of day, reach for 点.","tip":"几天 counts days; 几点 tells time."},
"wechat|群？":{"why":"群 qún literally means a crowd or flock, and on WeChat it is the group chat; 班 bān is a class, so 我们班的群 is \"our class group\". Asking is fine; now answer the offer. 拉我进去吧 lā wǒ jìnqù ba (pull me in) uses 进去 jìnqù, \"into there\", with 吧 ba as a friendly \"go ahead\".","tip":"A flock of chats: 群 qún is a WeChat group."},
"wechat|拉我？去哪儿？":{"why":"拉 lā means pull, and Chinese pictures adding someone to a group as pulling them in, so 拉你进群 lā nǐ jìn qún is \"pull you into the group\". Its opposite is 踢 tī (kick), for removing someone. 进 jìn (enter) plus 群 qún (group) gives the destination; asking 去哪儿 qù nǎr sounds like you think they want to drag you somewhere.","tip":"WeChat groups pull people in (拉) and kick people out (踢)."},
"hotpot|两位。要红锅。":{"why":"红锅 hóng guō is the all-red, chilli-oil broth, and you'd be eating straight Sichuan heat. 鸳鸯 yuānyang are mandarin ducks, which swim in pairs, so 鸳鸯锅 yuānyang guō is the split pot with spicy on one side and mild on the other. The second syllable of 鸳鸯 is often said lightly, yuānyang.","tip":"Two ducks, two broths: 鸳鸯锅."},
"hotpot|特辣！":{"why":"特 tè means extra or especially, so 特辣 tè là is the top spice level, often numbing hot. The scale goes 微辣 wēi là (slight), 中辣 zhōng là (medium), 特辣 tè là (extra), where 微 wēi means tiny. Even 微辣 in a Chongqing or Sichuan place is quite hot for most visitors, so the waiter will often double-check if you go higher.","tip":"微 wēi is \"micro\": 微辣 is the small dose."},
"hotpot|不辣。":{"why":"辣度 làdù means spice level, and the waiter was asking how spicy the red, 红锅 hóng guō, side should be, so 不辣 bú là doesn't fit his options. The non-spicy side is the clear broth, 清汤 qīngtāng. From his list, the lowest is 微辣 wēi là, mildly spicy.","tip":"Pick from the menu he reads you: 微辣 is the bottom rung."},
"hotpot|你给我们拿吧。":{"why":"自己 zìjǐ means oneself and 拿 ná means take or fetch, so 自己拿 zìjǐ ná is \"help yourself\". Hotpot places usually have a sauce bar, 调料台 tiáoliào tái, where you mix sesame paste, garlic, coriander and so on. 你给我们拿吧 nǐ gěi wǒmen ná ba asks him to fetch it for you, which he just said isn't how it works.","tip":"自己 zìjǐ = yourself: you walk to the sauce."},
"hotpot|要一个。":{"why":"Chinese counts with a number + measure word, and 个 ge works for aprons. There are two of you, so 两个 liǎng ge, using 两 liǎng (not 二 èr) before a measure word. 来 lái is the casual restaurant verb for \"bring\", so 来两个吧 lái liǎng ge ba is \"bring two, then\".","tip":"Count the people at the table, then 来 that many."},
"hotpot|我不脏。":{"why":"免得 miǎnde means \"so as to avoid\", and 弄脏 nòng zāng is \"to get (something) dirty\", 弄 nòng being a general \"make, cause\" verb. He's talking about your clothes, 衣服 yīfu, getting splashed with chilli oil later. Hotpot places hand out 围裙 wéiqún (aprons) because of this, so the answer is 要 yào (yes) and how many.","tip":"弄脏 is a future splash, not a comment on you."},
"hotpot|不冰的。":{"why":"不冰的 bù bīng de (not the iced one) works, but the word on menus and in shops is 常温 chángwēn, \"normal temperature\". 常 cháng means usual and 温 wēn means warmth. Many Chinese people prefer room-temperature drinks, especially with hot food, so this is a very common request.","tip":"常温 chángwēn: usual warmth, room temp."},
"hotpot|要。":{"why":"还是 háishi in a question offers a choice between A and B, so a yes or no answer doesn't fit. Note that 还是 háishi is for questions; in statements, \"or\" is 或者 huòzhě. Answer by repeating the option you want, like 常温的 chángwēn de, the 的 de meaning \"the one that's...\".","tip":"还是 = pick one; answer with the choice, not 要."},
"hotpot|我们是二个人。":{"why":"二 èr is the digit two, used for counting aloud, phone numbers and ordinals; 两 liǎng is \"a pair of\" before a measure word like 个 ge. So it's 两个人 liǎng ge rén, never 二个人. 是 shì is also optional; Chinese often says just 我们两个人 wǒmen liǎng ge rén, and 不是 bú shì first corrects his three.","tip":"Counting things? 两 + measure word."},
"hotpot|对，十五块。":{"why":"吧 ba at the end of 你们是三位吧 nǐmen shì sān wèi ba asks for confirmation, so 对 duì means \"yes, three\". 位 wèi is the polite measure word for people. He'll keep charging three sauce fees, and 十五块 shíwǔ kuài confirms you'll pay that. Start with 不是 bú shì (no) and give the right number, 两个人 liǎng ge rén.","tip":"Hear 吧? He's checking: correct with 不是 right away."},
"photo|拍我。":{"why":"拍 pāi literally means to pat or slap, and also to shoot a photo, so a bare 拍我 pāi wǒ to a stranger is blunt and, out of context, a little funny. 能…吗 néng… ma turns it into a polite \"could you...\", and 帮我 bāng wǒ (help me) frames it as a favour. 张 zhāng is the measure word for flat things like photos, so 拍张照 pāi zhāng zhào is \"take a photo\".","tip":"Wrap requests in 能帮我…吗？ for an instant polite favour."},
"photo|照片，可以吗？":{"why":"照片 zhàopiàn is a photo as an object, so the tourist had to guess you meant \"take one for me\". The action is 拍照 pāi zhào, and the favour is 帮我拍张照 bāng wǒ pāi zhāng zhào. Adding 能 néng and 吗 ma, 能帮我拍张照吗 néng bāng wǒ pāi zhāng zhào ma, makes it a clear, polite request.","tip":"照片 is the thing; 拍照 is the action."},
"photo|横的，只拍我。":{"why":"横 héng is horizontal (landscape) and 竖 shù is vertical (portrait), the same words used for the strokes in Chinese characters. 只 zhǐ means \"only\", so 只拍我 zhǐ pāi wǒ asks them to leave the tower out. 把 bǎ moves the object before the verb, and 拍进去 pāi jìnqù means \"shoot it into the frame\", so 把塔都拍进去 bǎ tǎ dōu pāi jìnqù gets the whole tower in, 都 dōu stressing \"all of it\".","tip":"横 lies down like landscape; 竖 stands up like portrait."},
"photo|茄子！":{"why":"茄子 qiézi (aubergine) is what Chinese photographers say instead of \"cheese\", because the ending -zi stretches your lips into a smile. It's the cue before the shot, not a reply afterwards. To thank them, praise the photo with 拍得真好 pāi de zhēn hǎo, where 得 de links the action to how well it was done, then 谢谢你 xièxie nǐ.","tip":"茄子 before the click; 拍得真好 after."},
"photo|拍我就行。":{"why":"怎么 zěnme before a verb asks \"how to\", so 怎么拍 zěnme pāi is about using your phone, not what to photograph. 按 àn means press with a finger, like a button or doorbell. 按这个白色的就行 àn zhège báisè de jiù xíng (just press this white one) solves their problem, and 就行 jiù xíng adds \"that's all it takes\".","tip":"怎么 + verb asks how; answer with the action, 按 àn."},
"photo|不要美。":{"why":"美 měi alone is the adjective beautiful, so 不要美 bú yào měi sounds like \"I don't want to be beautiful\". 美颜 měiyán is the beauty filter (literally beautify-face), standard on Chinese phones and often on by default. 不用了 bú yòng le politely declines, and 自然一点就好 zìrán yìdiǎn jiù hǎo means \"a bit natural is fine\".","tip":"美颜 měiyán = beauty + face, the filter."},
"photo|好的。":{"why":"开 kāi means switch on, so 要开美颜吗 yào kāi měiyán ma asks if you want the filter on, and 好的 hǎo de says yes. Saying 好的 to a question you didn't catch is a very common learner trap. 什么 shénme or 不好意思，再说一遍 bù hǎoyìsi, zài shuō yí biàn (sorry, once more) gets the question repeated.","tip":"Didn't catch it? Never 好的; ask 什么？"},
"photo|好吧。":{"why":"吧 ba softens or suggests, and 好吧 hǎo ba often carries a sigh, \"oh, all right then\", as if you're giving in. The friendly yes is 好啊 hǎo a, or even warmer, 当然可以 dāngrán kěyǐ, \"of course\". 当然 dāngrán means of course and 可以 kěyǐ means can, so it's an enthusiastic \"sure thing\".","tip":"好吧 sighs; 好啊 smiles; 当然可以 beams."},
"photo|不客气！":{"why":"不客气 bú kèqi answers 谢谢, but they didn't thank you, they asked for a favour. 也 yě (also) points back to you having had your photo taken, and 帮我们拍一张 bāng wǒmen pāi yì zhāng means \"take one for us\". 吧 ba makes it a gentle request, so the answer is a yes: 当然可以 dāngrán kěyǐ.","tip":"帮我们 = a favour request; reply 当然可以, not 不客气."},
"parcel|三千一百七十四。":{"why":"取件码 qǔjiànmǎ (pickup code) is an ID, like a locker number, so it's read digit by digit, never as an amount. Saying 三千一百七十四 sānqiān yìbǎi qīshísì makes the clerk translate it back into digits. In digit strings, 一 is said yāo so it isn't confused with 七 qī: 三一七四 sān yāo qī sì.","tip":"Codes are labels: read each digit, 1 as yāo."},
"parcel|三一七十。":{"why":"四 sì (four) and 十 shí (ten) differ in two ways: s versus sh, and falling tone versus rising tone. For 四 keep your tongue flat behind your teeth and drop the pitch sharply; for 十 curl it back and let the pitch rise. Even Chinese people mix these up, which is why there's a famous tongue-twister, 四是四，十是十 sì shì sì, shí shì shí.","tip":"四 sì falls flat; 十 shí curls and rises."},
"parcel|八八两零。":{"why":"两 liǎng means \"two of something\" and needs a measure word after it, like 两个 liǎng ge. When reading digits, as in phone numbers and codes, you use 二 èr. The clerk understands either way, but 八八二零 bā bā èr líng is what a local says.","tip":"Reading digits? 二 èr. Counting things? 两 liǎng."},
"parcel|前四位是一三九八。":{"why":"后 hòu means back or last and 前 qián means front or first, so 后四位 hòu sì wèi is the last four digits; 位 wèi here means a digit place. Parcel stations check the last four because the full number is printed on the label. Read them as digits: 八八二零 bā bā èr líng.","tip":"后 hòu is the tail end: the last four."},
"parcel|这不是我的。":{"why":"这不是我的 zhè bú shì wǒ de is a strong statement and the clerk will take the box back. If you're unsure, 我看一下 wǒ kàn yíxià (let me look) buys time to check the label. Here the clerk found the right box and just needs a signature, 签个字 qiān ge zì, so sign and say 好的，谢谢 hǎo de, xièxie.","tip":"Unsure? 我看一下 first; deny only when you're sure."},
"parcel|我是马修的。":{"why":"的 de shows possession, so 马修的 Mǎxiū de means \"Matthew's\", and 我是马修的 sounds like \"I belong to Matthew\". Drop 的 for a straight identity: 我是马修 wǒ shì Mǎxiū. 就 jiù adds emphasis, \"I'm the very one\", so 我就是马修 wǒ jiù shì Mǎxiū confirms it firmly.","tip":"的 makes things belong; you just ARE Matthew, no 的."},
"parcel|不是，我叫马修。":{"why":"In Chinese you answer the question as asked: she asked 是你本人吗 shì nǐ běnrén ma (is it you yourself), so 不是 bú shì means \"no, it isn't me\". Your next words then contradict that, and she may refuse to hand over the parcel. 本人 běnrén means the person themselves, a word you'll see on forms. Answer 是我 shì wǒ.","tip":"Answer with the question's own verb: 是你吗？ → 是我."},
"parcel|我可以。":{"why":"拿得动 ná de dòng is a potential complement: 拿 ná is carry, 动 dòng is move, and 得 de in between says \"able to\". 可以 kěyǐ is more about permission or being OK, so 我可以 wǒ kěyǐ is understandable but vague. Echoing the verb phrase, 拿得动 ná de dòng, is the natural yes.","tip":"Asked 拿得动吗？ Echo it: 拿得动."},
"parcel|拿不动，谢谢！":{"why":"In potential complements, 得 de and 不 bu sit in the same slot and flip the meaning: 拿得动 ná de dòng (can carry) versus 拿不动 ná bu dòng (can't carry). Both middle syllables are light and quick, so listen carefully. 拿不动，谢谢 ná bu dòng, xièxie sounds like you're asking for help, and he might start lifting it for you.","tip":"得 = can, 不 = can't, same middle slot."},
"parcel|为什么？":{"why":"为什么 wèi shénme on its own, with no context, can sound like you're challenging the clerk, who didn't set the charge. 到付 dàofù means pay on delivery, so the useful reply is a fact: 已经付过了 yǐjīng fù guo le, \"already paid\", where 过 guo marks something done before. Adding 在网上 zài wǎngshang (online) explains how.","tip":"State the fact, not the challenge: 已经付过了."},
"parcel|好，给你二十。":{"why":"Chinese numbers build like a formula: 十二 shí'èr is ten plus two = 12, while 二十 èrshí is two tens = 20. The order is the whole difference, so listen for which comes first. Also, if you'd paid online, handing over money means paying twice; say 我在网上已经付过了 wǒ zài wǎngshang yǐjīng fù guo le.","tip":"十 first = teens; 十 last = tens."}};
